(window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS=window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS||{})["m1-n101"]={"id":"n101","label":"Notes 10.1 · bold main idea first","revision":"ling406-modern-m1-n101-2","scripts":{"m1-lecture01-p001":[],"m1-lecture01-p002":[],"m1-lecture01-p003":[],"m1-lecture01-p004":[],"m1-lecture01-p005":[],"m1-lecture01-p006":[],"m1-lecture01-p007":[{"text":"**Alphabetic letters are not phonemes, because each language has its own rules for turning spelling into sound.** English spelling is especially ambiguous, or irregular. Even regular writing systems cause problems when languages are compared.","children":["**Pinyin** (a relatively recently invented system for writing Mandarin) is perfectly regular.","The phrase \"Wo qu tushuguan\" (我去图书馆) uses familiar letters, but English rules do not give its pronunciation.","The Chinese characters and the Pinyin write the same Mandarin phrase in two writing systems."]},{"text":"**French** spelling is also very regular.","children":["The sentence \"Comment tu t’appelles?\" also needs the French rules to be read aloud.","A writing system can be regular inside one language without being a sound code for all languages.","So comparing speech sounds must start from what speakers actually say."]}],"m1-lecture01-p008":[{"text":"**The International Phonetic Alphabet is a standard system for writing the sounds of all languages, showing their similarities and differences.** Its symbols stand for properties of pronunciation, not the spelling habits of one language.","children":["Sounds with different ordinary spellings can be compared directly.","Sounds that ordinary writing spells with one letter can be told apart."]},{"text":"The **IPA consonant chart** arranges symbols by how the sounds are produced.","children":["Rows include **plosive**, **nasal** and **fricative**.","Columns include **bilabial**, **alveolar** and **velar**.","Symbols in related positions stand for related ways of producing a sound.","So the chart is a system of comparisons, not a list of symbols to memorize."]}],"m1-lecture01-p009":[{"text":"**The IPA consonant chart is organized by three features, which are place, manner and voicing.** A consonant can match another on two features and differ on the third.","children":["**Place** (where the vocal tract is constricted) runs across the chart from front to back.","**Manner** (the degree of closure) runs down the chart from closed to open.","**Voicing** (when vocal fold vibration starts relative to the closure) shows as pairs of symbols."]},{"text":"Each feature has a clear case on the chart.","children":["Bilabial sounds sit at the left, because the constriction is at the lips.","Velar sounds and sounds made farther back sit farther right.","A **plosive** has a complete closure. A **fricative** has a narrower passage.","Voicing can start before or during the closure, or after it.","In a pair, the right-hand symbol is voiced. So p and b share the bilabial plosive position."]}],"m1-lecture01-p010":[{"text":"**/p/ and /b/ have the same place and manner of articulation but differ in voicing.** Features describe sound similarities and differences in a relatively precise and well-defined way.","children":["/p/ is plosive, bilabial and not voiced.","/b/ is plosive, bilabial and voiced.","Both are bilabial plosives, so the lips close and then release.","They differ in **voicing** (the timing of phonation relative to the release of the closure)."]},{"text":"The same difference separates **/t/ and /d/**.","children":["That pair is made at a different place.","It still pairs a voiceless stop with a voiced stop.","So features allow general statements about **voiced stops** such as /b/, /d/ and /g/."]}],"m1-lecture01-p011":[{"text":"**The voiced and unvoiced stop contrast is produced differently across languages and across contexts within a language.** The IPA writes these differences compactly.","children":["**[pʰ]** is plosive, bilabial, not voiced and **aspirated** (followed by a puff of air after the release).","**[p]** is plosive, bilabial, not voiced and not aspirated.","**[b]** is plosive, bilabial and voiced."]},{"text":"English and French build the contrast from different pairs.","children":["In English, word-initially, \"peer\" often starts with [pʰ] and \"beer\" often starts with unaspirated [p].","A hand in front of the mouth makes the puff easy to feel.","In most varieties of French, the contrast is really [p] in \"pou\" against [b] in \"boue\".","So the labels voiced and unvoiced do not mean the same timing in every language."]}],"m1-lecture01-p012":[{"text":"**Slashes mark broad transcription of phonemes, and square brackets mark narrow transcription with as much detail as possible.** The two notations answer different questions about the same utterance.","children":["**Broad transcription** writes the phonemes of an utterance, such as /p/ and /b/.","It keeps the distinctions that organize words in the language."]},{"text":"**Narrow transcription** records how a sound was actually produced, such as [pʰ], [p] and [b].","children":["[pʰ] is marked aspirated, and [p] is marked not aspirated.","It can keep a difference like aspiration even when that difference does not distinguish words."]},{"text":"Two broad labels sit beside three narrow forms.","children":["More phonetic detail does not create more phonemes.","So a **phone** (an actual speech sound) and a **phoneme** (a contrastive category) are not the same."]}],"m1-lecture01-p013":[{"text":"**Consonant features are anchored to how speech is produced, and place of articulation names where the vocal tract is constricted.** The lips can close together. Parts of the tongue can approach or touch different regions of the mouth.","children":["These movements change where the outgoing air is blocked or narrowed."]},{"text":"Three places use different articulators.","children":["**Bilabial** means the upper and lower lips close at the front of the mouth.","**Alveolar** means the tongue tip rises toward the ridge behind the upper teeth.","**Velar** means the back of the tongue rises toward the soft palate."]},{"text":"Place is separate from manner.","children":["A complete closure can happen at several different places.","So finding the place means locating the main constriction, not reading the letter."]}],"m1-lecture01-p014":[{"text":"**Consonant features describe both the route of the airflow and which articulators make the constriction.** All of these arrangements happen in the same vocal tract.","children":["A **nasal** consonant lets air out through the nose while the mouth is closed at some point.","The passage between mouth and nose is open, so air keeps flowing despite the closure.","For a nasal at the lips, the lips are closed and the route to the nasal cavity is open.","So a nasal can share its place with a stop and differ in the airflow route."]},{"text":"Two places involve the teeth at the front of the mouth.","children":["**Labiodental** means the lower lip meets the upper teeth.","**Dental** means the tongue is at the front teeth.","Tracking the airflow and locating the constriction are separate steps."]}],"m1-lecture01-p015":[{"text":"**Vowels are organized by height, by front or back tongue position, and by lip rounding.** The vowel chart runs from close at the top to open at the bottom, and from front at the left to back at the right.","children":["**Close** vowels tend to have a higher jaw position and tongue than **open** vowels.","The tongue is generally farther forward for **front** vowels than for **back** vowels.","**Rounded** vowels have rounding of the lips. In a pair, the right-hand symbol is rounded."]},{"text":"**/y/** is the vowel in French \"tu\" and Mandarin \"qu\" (去).","children":["It can sound like /u/ to English speakers.","/i/ and /y/ share the close front region and differ only in rounding.","Making an /i/ and rounding the lips changes it toward /y/.","The tongue does not move back as for /u/, so lip shape is separate from tongue position."]}],"m1-lecture01-p016":[{"text":"**A vowel category is a pattern of articulation, not one fixed tongue shape.** The whole vocal tract shapes the sound, not the tongue alone.","children":["The center of the **tongue body** gives one reference point per vowel.","That point captures one important difference among vowels.","Whole tongue outlines can differ in more than that one point."]},{"text":"Some vowels are very **dynamic**.","children":["The **diphthongs** (vowels that change during their production) in \"boat\" and \"bait\" move through time.","A single point cannot give the path the tongue takes.","So a dynamic vowel is understood by following its change over its duration."]}],"m1-lecture01-p017":[{"text":"**Speakers of the same language can differ in which vowels they distinguish.** Even within American English, vowels are very salient and differ across **dialects**.","children":["The test case is the two vowels in the lower back corner of the chart, rounded /ɔ/ and open back /ɑ/."]},{"text":"The two kinds of speaker give opposite results for \"caught\" and \"cot\".","children":["A mid-Atlantic speaker from the outer boroughs of New York City distinguishes the two vowels.","For that speaker, \"caught\" and \"cot\" are different words.","Most people from other parts of the country do not distinguish them.","For them, \"caught\" and \"cot\" are **homophones** (words that sound the same)."]},{"text":"Both are English, so the difference is a systematic dialect pattern.","children":["Whether a speaker has the contrast is found by listening to that speaker."]}],"m1-lecture01-p018":[{"text":"**The lack of invariance problem is that one speech category sounds physically different across speakers and contexts, yet listeners still understand it.** The problem comes up again and again in speech perception.","children":["Some people say \"Aaron\" and \"Erin\" with the same vowel, and listeners who keep them apart still understand.","A listener can hear such a difference for some time and only notice once attention is drawn to it."]},{"text":"Measured vowels give the same problem.","children":["Each vowel is placed by its **first formant (F1)** and **second formant (F2)**, in cycles per second.","Many productions of one vowel spread over a wide region.","The regions for neighboring vowels overlap.","So no single pair of F1 and F2 values identifies a vowel."]}],"m1-lecture01-p019":[{"text":"**A minimal pair is two words whose difference in meaning depends on one sound, and it is the evidence that two phones are different phonemes.** Two phones are **contrastive** when swapping them changes the meaning. Contrastive phones are different phonemes.","children":["The two low back vowels had to be established with two words that differ only in those vowels."]},{"text":"\"Caught\" and \"cot\" work this way for one kind of speaker.","children":["For a speaker who distinguishes the vowels, \"caught\" and \"cot\" are a minimal pair.","The consonants stay the same, so the vowel alone separates the words.","For a speaker who merges the vowels, the pair sounds the same.","So that pair does not establish two vowel phonemes for that speaker.","The evidence is a difference in meaning, not just a difference in sound."]}],"m1-lecture01-p020":[{"text":"**Minimal pairs show that several English stop contrasts all depend on the same feature, voicing.** The evidence comes from comparing minimal pairs in each language.","children":["**/b/ and /p/** are bilabial stops that differ in voicing.","**/t/ and /d/** are alveolar stops that differ in voicing.","**/k/ and /g/** are velar stops that differ in voicing."]},{"text":"Each pair keeps place and stop manner the same.","children":["Voicing changes within each pair.","Place changes across the pairs.","So one feature describes all three contrasts.","Two phones existing physically does not make them a contrast in every language."]}],"m1-lecture01-p021":[{"text":"**Allophones are different phones that are different realizations of the same phoneme.** There is a great deal of variability in how phonemes are realized. Some of it is very predictable.","children":["The variation depends on position and neighboring sounds, so it is systematic.","It does not change which word is being said."]},{"text":"Recording three phrases in **Praat** (a program for recording and analyzing speech) compares /p/ in three contexts.","children":["In \"pot that\", /p/ starts the word before the vowel.","In \"spot that\", /p/ comes after /s/.","In \"stop that\", /p/ ends the word.","The release and aspiration can differ, while each one is still /p/."]}],"m1-lecture01-p022":[{"text":"**A recording of \"pot that\", \"spot that\" and \"stop that\" makes the allophones of /p/ visible over time.** The contexts differ, so closure, release, aspiration and the following voiced sound can differ in timing.","children":["These differences fit with all three words containing the same phoneme /p/."]},{"text":"The recording is read in two forms.","children":["The **waveform** (top) gives the amplitude of the signal over time.","The **spectrogram** (bottom) gives acoustic energy over time and frequency.","Time runs left to right in both, so the same event lines up vertically.","Whether two phones are different phonemes depends on whether the difference distinguishes words."]}],"m1-lecture01-p023":[{"text":"**Unreleased [p̚] and aspirated [pʰ] at the end of \"stop\" are allophones, because no minimal pair separates them.** Speakers sometimes say [stɑpʰ].","children":["This happens when stressing the word at the end of a sentence.","It also happens in careful speech, called **citation form**.","There is no difference in meaning between [stɑp̚] and [stɑpʰ].","Allo means different, so allophones are different phones for the same phoneme."]},{"text":"The forms also alternate across **morphological forms** of the word.","children":["\"Stop\", \"stops\", \"stopped\" and \"stopping\" each put something different after the /p/.","That changes the environment of the /p/.","So whether the /p/ is released and aspirated depends on the form.","A difference that can be heard is not always a phoneme difference."]}],"m1-lecture01-p024":[{"text":"**The word voicing is used in two different ways, one for phones and one for phonemes.** That is why it can be confusing.","children":["In **narrow transcription** (describing specific phones), a sound is voiced if the vocal folds vibrate during the whole gesture or sound.","This is a claim about one actual production."]},{"text":"In **broad transcription** (describing phonemes), voicing is **realized** in a number of different ways.","children":["One way is presence or absence of **aspiration** at the beginnings of words.","Another way is the relative duration of voicing to closure in \"writer\" and \"rider\".","So one phoneme contrast can have more than one physical cue.","Ask which level is meant when a sound is called voiced."]}],"m1-lecture01-p025":[{"text":"**Phonotactics is, roughly, which phonemes can occur together in syllable structures, and it reshapes clusters that English does not allow.** Rules about syllable structure turn out to be complex. They still seem to shape speech production.","children":["The name Sbarro comes out as [spɑroʊ] or [zbɑroʊ].","It never comes out as [sbɑroʊ], whatever speakers think they are saying."]},{"text":"Foreign names force speakers to deal with such clusters.","children":["Speakers tend to insert vowels.","The Polish name Zbigniew becomes [zɨ’bIg.nʲu].","The Kru name Gbagbo becomes [gə.bɑ.gə.boʊ].","The changes follow English syllable patterns, not an inability to say each consonant."]}],"m1-lecture01-p026":[{"text":"**Phonemes are not letters, and they are not even specific sounds or gestures.** A phoneme is \"the smallest phonetic unit in a language that is capable of conveying a distinction in meaning.\" This can be complicated in practice.","children":["A phoneme is defined by the contrasts it makes, which ties it to minimal pairs.","One phoneme can have several allophones."]},{"text":"Three things belong to three descriptions.","children":["A **letter** is part of a writing system.","A **phone** is an actual speech realization.","A **phoneme** organizes contrasts that carry meaning."]},{"text":"The **International Phonetic Alphabet** lets speech scientists communicate about similarities and differences between sounds across languages.","children":["So it is easy to think of it as an alphabet of phonemes.","That would be a mistake, because phonemes are not letters."]}],"m1-lecture01-p027":[{"text":"**Speech is a continuously changing signal, not an orderly stream of letter-like units.** Literate people easily imagine speech as a row of letters, but it is nothing like that.","children":["A **spectrogram** of \"A sore back is a real pain\" makes this visible.","Time runs left to right, and frequency rises upward.","Darker regions have stronger acoustic energy."]},{"text":"The patterns flow into each other.","children":["Frequency patterns change over time, and transitions connect neighboring segments.","The labels and boundary markers are added on top and do not cut the signal into pieces.","Phonemic categories do not match isolated blocks with one fixed acoustic shape.","So recognizing the sentence means interpreting changing patterns."]}],"m1-lecture01-p028":[{"text":"**Speech is made by coordinated movements of the lips, jaw, tongue and other parts of the vocal tract.** Their changing configuration shapes the sound that comes out.","children":["A side view from USC SPAN shows the articulators in action.","It locates the tongue in the mouth and the passage toward the throat."]},{"text":"Each articulator does a different job.","children":["The **tongue** changes the inner shape of the oral cavity.","The **lips and jaw** change its opening.","The movements run as one ongoing sequence.","So a speech **gesture** is part of a moving system, not one posture repeated for each phoneme."]}],"m1-lecture01-p029":[{"text":"**Hockett (1955) compared hearing to identifying smashed eggs, because speech mixes its units together and the hearer only has to identify them.** The analogy has three stages.","children":["Unboiled Easter eggs of various sizes and colors ride a moving belt.","This flow stands for the impulses from the **phoneme source**."]},{"text":"A wringer smashes the eggs and rubs them more or less into each other.","children":["The mess stands for the output of the **speech transmitter**."]},{"text":"An inspector studies the yolks, the spread-out albumen and the bits of shell.","children":["He decides what flow of eggs reached the wringer.","The inspector stands for the **hearer**.","He does not rebuild the eggs, which is physically impossible. He only identifies them."]}],"m1-lecture01-p030":[{"text":"**People have started to question whether phonemes are real, either psychologically or linguistically.** A phoneme is useful for describing contrasts. That does not settle how words are stored or how speech is recognized.","children":["**Lotto and Holt (2000)** wrote about the illusion of the phoneme.","**Beckman and Edwards (2000)** wrote about how phonological categories develop and the primacy of lexical learning.","**Port (2007)** asked how words are stored in memory, beyond phones and phonemes.","**Goldinger and Azuma (2003)** wrote about puzzle-solving science in speech perception."]},{"text":"The shared question is about mental representation.","children":["A descriptive notation and a model of mental processing answer different questions.","So the open issue is whether phoneme-sized units describe the mind itself."]}],"m1-lecture01-p031":[],"m1-lecture01-p032":[],"m1-lecture03-p001":[],"m1-lecture03-p002":[],"m1-lecture03-p003":[],"m1-lecture03-p004":[{"text":"**The form knifes is a real word because knife can be a verb, and the verb takes a different ending than the noun.** The case comes from an argument over Scrabble.","children":["One player says knifes is not a word because the plural of knife is knives.","The other answers that Tony knifes Bernardo in West Side Story."]},{"text":"Three ideas explain it.","children":["**Inflectional morphology** (changing a word's form for grammar) gives the plural **knives** from the noun.","**Derivational morphology** (making a new word from an existing one) gives the verb knife from the noun. This case is **zero derivation** (a change in part of speech with no affix added).","**Phrases and clauses** set the verb's ending. \"He knifes him\" is a singular noun, a verb and a noun, so the verb needs the **third person singular** form."]},{"text":"Knives is the noun's plural. Knifes is the verb's form after he.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture03-p005":[{"text":"**Derivational morphology derives new words from existing parts, and those parts are called morphemes.** A **morpheme** contributes meaning or grammatical information.","children":["A morpheme is not a syllable.","A morpheme is not a single speech sound."]},{"text":"The word **morphology** has a fairly transparent derivation.","children":["The part morph- (from Greek, meaning form) comes first.","The part -ology (also from Greek, here meaning something like the study of) comes second.","Together they mean \"the study of the forms of words\"."]},{"text":"A similar process gives other unit names.","children":["**Morpheme** is the unit of word form.","**Phoneme** uses phone, another Greek morpheme (probably meaning speech).","A unit in text should get a name built the same way.","Graphology looks like it should name the study of those units, but the word does not mean that."]}],"m1-lecture03-p006":[{"text":"**A word made by derivation has a meaning that is only partly predictable from its base.** **Derivational morphology** is **productive** (able to make new words), but only in limited ways.","children":["Made-up words are hard to guess the meaning of.","The meaning of the final form is only **partially predictable**."]},{"text":"**Zero derivation** (changing the part of speech with no affix) usually works with a **narrow sense** of the base word.","children":["The noun knife covers many objects. Image search results for knife include several cutting tools and tableware.","A search for prison shiv gives an improvised weapon.","The verb knife covers one kind of action, which is attacking somebody with a knife.","The weapon-like knives, such as the prison shiv, are the ones a person would use to knife somebody.","A table knife is a knife, but it is not a natural fit for the verb."]}],"m1-lecture03-p007":[{"text":"**The suffix -hood is productive, but it does not apply to every stem and does not always do the same job.** **Derivational morphology** is limited in how widely and how regularly **affixes** (parts added to a stem) are applied.","children":["The pattern is a stem plus -hood.","The result is a state of being that is associated with the stem noun."]},{"text":"Some words follow the pattern.","children":["**Brotherhood** and **sisterhood** fit it.","**Knighthood** fits it oddly. Two brothers share their brotherhood, but each knight has his own knighthood."]},{"text":"Some words do not follow the pattern.","children":["**Neighborhood** is a perfectly good word, but -hood does a different job in it entirely.","**Friendhood** is not used. English says friendship instead, for no apparent reason.","**Enemyhood** is not used, though maybe it ought to be. The available words are less common ones such as enmity or antagonism."]},{"text":"Some of these words come from Liberman and Prince's introductory linguistics course at Penn.","children":["Knowing an affix includes knowing which words actually take it."]}],"m1-lecture03-p008":[{"text":"**Zero derivation leaves the word's form alone, but the verb's meaning can be far from the noun's meaning.** **Zero derivation** changes a word's category with no visible affix. It still does complicated things with **semantics** (meaning).","children":["Dish, spoon, fork and table are strong associates of the noun knife.","Their meanings as verbs are **not similar**."]},{"text":"Each sentence uses a learned meaning.","children":["\"She can dish it out but she can’t take it\" uses dish as a verb.","\"He was born with a silver spoon and a forked tongue in his mouth\" uses forked for a shape, with an established figurative meaning.","\"They decided to table the issue after a day of inconclusive negotiations\" uses table as a verb."]},{"text":"None of these is a literal reference to a utensil or to furniture.","children":["The meaning comes from **conventional usage**, not from naming the object.","The silver spoon expression is the kind of thing studied in **Cognitive Linguistics**."]}],"m1-lecture03-p009":[{"text":"**Adding an affix can change how the rest of the word is pronounced, especially where the stress falls.** **Derivational morphology** more commonly changes the pronunciation and spelling of a word than leaves them alone.","children":["Certificate is /sər.ˈtI.fɨ.kɨt/.","Certification is /ˌsər.tɨ.fɨ.ˈkeI.ʃən/.","Recertification is /ˈri.sər.tɨ.fɨ.ˌkeI.ʃən/.","The **stress** sits on different syllables in the three words, and some vowels differ too."]},{"text":"This effect on **phonology** (the sound pattern of a word) is one reason for \"The Chaos\" of English spelling.","children":["Higher fidelity to the spoken form does not necessarily improve readability."]},{"text":"Some noun and verb pairs differ mainly in **stress**.","children":["Record is stressed differently as a noun and as a verb.","Invite is stressed differently in \"He did not receive an invite\" and \"We did not invite him\".","Entrance is stressed differently too, but its noun and verb are not actually related to each other."]}],"m1-lecture03-p010":[{"text":"**Inflectional morphology changes a word in a predictable way and does not make a new dictionary word.** English has three inflections here.","children":["**Plural** gives knife(n) and knives, one fork and two forks, one mouse and a lot of mice.","**Third person singular present** gives knife(v) and knifes, I walk and she walks, you run and he runs.","**Past** gives knife(v) and knifed, and today I walk and yesterday I walked. It also gives today she sings and in the past, she sang."]},{"text":"Menn writes that “...we’d think it was silly to have separate dictionary entries for the singular and plural forms of nouns.”","children":["The singular and the plural are forms of one word."]},{"text":"The phonological process of inflecting words often has some **irregularity**.","children":["Regular forms add an ending, as in forks and walked.","Irregular forms change the word, as in mice and sang.","Both kinds give the same grammatical meaning.","Irregularity becomes important for language learning models and for kinds of developmental language impairment."]}],"m1-lecture03-p011":[{"text":"**Syntax can require a word to be inflected, but it does not require a word to be derived.** **Inflectional morphology** depends on the **syntax** (how words are organized into phrases and sentences) of the rest of the sentence.","children":["The phrase \"two [noun]+pl\" has a slot for a noun.","Whatever noun goes there must be pluralized somehow.","A regular noun gives two forks.","An irregular noun gives two knives."]},{"text":"**Derivational morphology** has no syntactic process like this, even for very productive forms.","children":["The closest case is a formula, “Once you [verb] it, you can un-[verb] it.”","That formula is not syntax.","No sentence requires a verb to have an un- form."]}],"m1-lecture03-p012":[{"text":"**English uses inflection in only a few places, and many other languages use it for much more.** English **inflection** covers plurals, the third person singular present, and the past.","children":["Plurals are knives, two forks and a lot of mice.","Third person singular present forms are knifes, she walks and he runs.","Past forms are knifed, walked and sang."]},{"text":"Many languages inflect more than English.","children":["Many languages conjugate verbs much more elaborately for **person**.","Many languages do the same for **tense**.","Many languages form the **plural** differently for small and large numbers.","Many languages have **case markings** (noun markings that encode information about syntactic or semantic roles)."]}],"m1-lecture03-p013":[{"text":"**Derivation creates new words, and inflection gives a word the form its sentence requires.** Both kinds of morphology share two properties.","children":["Both create new phonological forms out of old parts.","Both change the meanings of base forms."]},{"text":"**Derivational morphology** has two properties of its own.","children":["It creates **new words**.","It is productive, but not always, and typically in a limited way."]},{"text":"**Inflectional morphology** has three properties of its own.","children":["It changes how words interact syntactically with other words.","It is **highly productive**, but not always regular.","It is **required by syntax**."]},{"text":"The knife forms give one case of each.","children":["The verb knife from the noun knife is derivation.","The form knifes after he is inflection."]}],"m1-lecture03-p014":[{"text":"**Words combine into phrases, and phrases use function words as well as content words.** Above the word are **phrases**, **clauses** and **sentences**.","children":["**Content words** include nouns and verbs.","Other content word classes are modifiers such as adjectives and adverbs.","**Function words** come in once more than a couple of words are put together.","Determiners and prepositions are function words that organize how content words relate."]},{"text":"The sentence “The video for “More than Words” came out before most of you were born” contains two **noun phrases**.","children":["The first is The video for “More than Words”.","The second is most of you.","They differ in length, so a noun phrase is not a fixed number of words."]}],"m1-lecture03-p015":[{"text":"**The video for “More than Words” is a noun phrase built from a determiner, a noun and a prepositional phrase.** Its structure is written **Det + N + PP**.","children":["**The** is the **determiner** (the function word that begins the noun phrase).","**Video** is the head noun.","**For** is the **preposition** (the function word that begins the prepositional phrase).","The prepositional phrase lets the song title specify which video is meant."]},{"text":"The title “More than Words” is not really part of the sentence.","children":["It is being used like a noun.","With the quotes removed, it is not a phrase.","It is then a bunch of words strung together in a row."]}],"m1-lecture03-p016":[{"text":"**Phrases with completely different words can share the one abstract structure Det + N + PP.** The structure is incredibly **abstract** because it names categories, not words.","children":["\"two guys from the early ‘nineties\" is two, then guys, then from the early ‘nineties.","\"a band with only one hit\" is a, then band, then with only one hit.","\"one and only one phrase unrelated to early ‘nineties one-hit wonder Extreme\" is one and only one, then phrase, then the rest."]},{"text":"The parts differ inside.","children":["A **determiner** can be several words, as in one and only one.","The **head nouns** are guys, band and phrase.","The first two modifiers are **prepositional phrases** beginning with from and with.","The third modifier begins with the adjective unrelated, followed by a to phrase.","That third modifier is an adjective phrase with a prepositional phrase inside it."]}],"m1-lecture03-p017":[{"text":"**Abstract categories such as noun phrase are needed to compare different phrases and different languages.** The abstraction leaves out the individual words.","children":["Without it, very different surface forms could not be called similar.","Without it, similarities and differences between languages could not be stated coherently."]},{"text":"All languages have **NPs** (noun phrases), but they can form them very differently.","children":["English has the cat in the coffee shop, with a determiner first and the PP after the noun.","Chinese has 咖啡店裡面的小貓, glossed as coffeeshop inside + cat.","The Chinese order is **PP + N**.","The Chinese NP has **no determiner**."]},{"text":"The missing determiner helps explain a learning problem.","children":["Speakers of Chinese languages find determiners very difficult to learn in a second language (L2)."]}],"m1-lecture03-p018":[{"text":"**Every prepositional phrase here is a preposition followed by a noun phrase, written P + NP.** Breaking down three **prepositional phrases** gives the general structure.","children":["From the early ‘nineties is from plus the NP the early ‘nineties. That NP is Det + Adj + N.","With only one hit is with plus the NP only one hit. That NP is given as P + Det + N.","Unrelated to early ‘nineties one-hit wonder Extreme is unrelated to plus an NP. That NP is given as Adv + Adj + Adv + Adj + N."]},{"text":"The **NP** inside can be short or long.","children":["It still counts as one unit.","The preposition relates that whole unit to the rest of the expression."]}],"m1-lecture03-p019":[{"text":"**Phrase structure is hierarchical, which means smaller phrases sit inside larger ones.** The phrase from the early ‘nineties has several levels.","children":["The determiner, the adjective and the noun are inside the **noun phrase**.","The noun phrase is inside the **prepositional phrase**.","The prepositional phrase is inside a sentence, or inside yet another noun phrase."]},{"text":"Curly brackets and parentheses in a flat row are a clumsy way to write these levels.","children":["**Trees** are diagrams that give each phrase a node above the parts it contains.","Hierarchy is part of why people started using trees to diagram sentences.","Hierarchy is also an important feature of languages."]}],"m1-lecture03-p020":[{"text":"**A verb phrase is a verb plus the phrases that complete it, and those phrases vary.** The **verb phrase** came out before most of you were born is **V + PP**.","children":["The V is came out.","The PP is before most of you were born.","The PP is hard to unpack because most of you were born looks like a verb phrase.","It specifies a point in time that complements before."]},{"text":"Many verb phrases also contain **noun phrases**.","children":["Sang the song is **V + NP**.","Worked himself to death is **V + NP + PP**, with himself as the NP and to death as the PP.","Threw a party for him is **V + NP + PP**, with a party as the NP and for him as the PP."]}],"m1-lecture03-p021":[{"text":"**A clause is a verb phrase together with its subject, and the subject is the noun phrase that controls the verb.** The sentence “The video for “More than Words” came out before most of you were born” has two parts.","children":["The NP is The video for “More than Words”, which is Det + N + PP.","The VP is came out before most of you were born, which is V + PP."]},{"text":"The **clause** pairs that VP with its **subject**.","children":["The whole first NP is the subject.","The **head noun** is video.","The whole sentence is **one clause**."]}],"m1-lecture03-p022":[{"text":"**A verb agrees with the head noun of its own subject, even when another noun is closer.** This agreement is what **controls** means.","children":["“The plums that were in the icebox were delicious” is a simple sentence with one clause.","Its parts are given as Det + N + PP and then VP.","The verb agrees with plums, not icebox, because plums is the **head** of the noun phrase."]},{"text":"“The plums in the icebox, which you were probably saving for breakfast, were delicious” is a **clause within a clause**, set off with commas.","children":["You is the subject of were probably saving for breakfast.","Plums is the subject of were delicious.","Changing you to my roommate changes were probably saving to was probably saving.","Were delicious does not change, because its subject is still plums."]},{"text":"\"Forgive me\" has an **implicit subject** (a subject that is understood but not said).","children":["The speaker wants whoever shares his icebox to forgive him for eating the plums."]}],"m1-lecture03-p023":[{"text":"**The same event can be said with different subjects, so the subject is not always the one who acted.** Two sentences mean the same thing but are very different **syntactically**.","children":["The subject is the phrase the verb agrees with."]},{"text":"\"I have eaten the plums in the icebox\" is the **active** sentence.","children":["I is the **subject** and appears first.","A verb phrase follows.","The plums in the icebox is an NP inside the verb phrase. It is the **object** of the verb."]},{"text":"\"The plums in the icebox were eaten by me\" is the **passive** sentence.","children":["The plums in the icebox becomes the **subject**.","The verb were eaten agrees with the plural noun, which is the evidence.","By me is just a **PP** that fills out the VP."]}],"m1-lecture03-p024":[{"text":"**Semantic roles stay the same in the active and passive sentences, and only the syntactic roles change.** **Semantic roles** (what each participant does in the event) do the job of telling \"who did what to whom\". **Syntactic roles** (subject, object) do that job less.","children":["The **agent** is the eater.","The **undergoer** is the plums.","The **location** is the icebox."]},{"text":"The passive order is Undergoer + Location + Verb + Agent.","children":["The plums is the undergoer, in the icebox is the location, and by me is the agent.","The subject is the undergoer."]},{"text":"The active order is Agent + Verb + Undergoer + Location.","children":["I is the agent, the plums is the undergoer, and in the icebox is the location.","The subject is the agent."]},{"text":"English usually aligns the syntactic subject with the agent.","children":["That habit is why it feels counterintuitive that me is not the subject of the passive sentence.","The relationship between syntactic and semantic roles also influences sentence processing."]}],"m1-lecture03-p025":[{"text":"**Language is highly generative, and speakers use that ability mostly without thinking about it.** **Generative** means familiar parts can be combined into new words and new sentences.","children":["**Derivational morphology** makes new words.","**Syntax** organizes words into clauses, clauses into phrases, and phrases into sentences.","**Inflectional morphology** modifies words for different syntactic roles."]},{"text":"People do all of this mostly without explicit thought.","children":["A speaker can form a plural or build a phrase without naming a grammatical category.","One of the hardest things to understand about linguistics is what there is to explain.","Some of the most complex phenomena are hiding in plain sight."]}],"m1-lecture03-p026":[],"m1-lecture03-p027":[],"m1-lecture04-p001":[],"m1-lecture04-p002":[{"text":"**Association norms record which words people produce when they see a cue word.** **Association norms** (summaries of the responses people give to a cue) are collected with a simple task.","children":["A participant sees a word on a screen for a few seconds.","The participant writes down the first few words that come to mind.","The task asks for immediate associations, not a definition."]},{"text":"Responses are then compared across people.","children":["The same cue can bring up different associates in different people.","Some associations recur across many people.","Other responses are unusual."]}],"m1-lecture04-p003":[{"text":"**LION is a cue, and the participant supplies the words it brings to mind.**","children":["The responses reveal connections in **lexical knowledge** (what a person knows about words).","TIGER is one possible response.","TIGER would connect two animal concepts.","No participant has to give TIGER."]},{"text":"The cue starts the response.","children":["The association itself comes from the participant."]}],"m1-lecture04-p004":[{"text":"**TRIPE looks like STRIPES, but the two words do not share a meaning.**","children":["The two words overlap in **form** (spelling and sound).","They do not overlap in **meaning**.","Similar form and connected meaning are different relationships."]},{"text":"The pair makes the **arbitrariness** of form and meaning concrete.","children":["Overlapping letters can occur in words with very different meanings.","The form of a word does not tell you what it means."]}],"m1-lecture04-p005":[{"text":"**FREE is a cue, and the participant generates the response instead of choosing from a list.**","children":["The participant writes words that come to mind from FREE.","No list of answers is offered."]},{"text":"Recording the response makes an internal association available to study.","children":["Responses to FREE can then be compared across people.","The norm for FREE comes from what many people produce on their own."]}],"m1-lecture04-p006":[{"text":"**STRIPES names a property, so its associates can be a different kind of thing.**","children":["STRIPES is a **visual property** (a feature you can see, here a pattern).","An association can link a property to an animal that has it.","STRIPES and TIGER can be associated.","STRIPES names a pattern, while TIGER names an animal."]},{"text":"This link is not **similarity** (sharing properties).","children":["LION and TIGER are similar animals.","STRIPES and TIGER are associated but not similar."]}],"m1-lecture04-p007":[{"text":"**PEAR and BEAR look alike in form but name different things.**","children":["PEAR names a fruit.","BEAR names an animal.","Their forms overlap."]},{"text":"The pair separates two relationships.","children":["A **formal relationship** (shared sound or spelling) links PEAR and BEAR.","A **semantic relationship** (shared meaning) does not link them.","A shared spelling pattern is not evidence of similar meaning."]}],"m1-lecture04-p008":[{"text":"**BEAR is another cue whose form overlaps with PEAR while its meaning does not.**","children":["BEAR resembles PEAR in form.","BEAR does not resemble PEAR in meaning."]},{"text":"**Association norms** record the responses people actually give.","children":["The experimenter does not decide in advance whether a response will be based on form or meaning.","The norm captures whatever connection the cue brings to mind."]}],"m1-lecture04-p009":[{"text":"**TREE is a cue, and its norm summarizes the different associates that different people give.**","children":["The first responses show which words are readily connected with TREE.","That evidence holds for the task context it came from."]},{"text":"The cue stays the same physical word.","children":["Different people can supply different associates.","The **norm** (the summary of all responses) does not treat one response as the only association."]}],"m1-lecture04-p010":[{"text":"**TIGER links to LION through similar meaning and to STRIPES through a characteristic pattern.**","children":["TIGER and LION are related as members of the same animal category.","TIGER and STRIPES are related as an animal and one of its properties."]},{"text":"Both kinds of link can be part of a **lexical network** (a network of connected word representations).","children":["The two links are different relationships.","So association and similarity are not interchangeable."]}],"m1-lecture04-p011":[],"m1-lecture04-p012":[{"text":"**LION, TIGER and STRIPES are tabulated because they show two kinds of meaning link that matter later.**","children":["LION and TIGER name similar animals.","TIGER and STRIPES link an animal with a characteristic property.","Their association can be measured by counting how often participants give one word in response to another."]},{"text":"The pairs TRIPE and STRIPES, and PEAR and BEAR, make a separate point.","children":["Their forms overlap.","Their meanings do not.","The link between a word's form and its meaning is largely **arbitrary** (not predictable from the form)."]},{"text":"Three relationships stay separate.","children":["One is similarity of form.","One is similarity of meaning.","One is the tendency of one word to bring another to mind."]}],"m1-lecture04-p013":[{"text":"**Being associated is different from being similar, and norms depend on how the task was set up.** **Association** is a tendency for one item to bring another to mind. **Similarity** is about shared properties.","children":["TIGER and STRIPES are associated.","TIGER and STRIPES are not similar objects."]},{"text":"Participants may have used a deliberate **strategy**.","children":["A strategy can change which words they write."]},{"text":"The **list structure** could influence the answers.","children":["A list of only wild animals could encourage one set of responses.","A list of only household appliances could encourage a different set.","A norm is evidence about responses under specific conditions."]}],"m1-lecture04-p014":[{"text":"**A few metaphors from neuroscience are the basis for most theory and experimental design in psycholinguistics.** Menn's chapter is called \"How Brains Work\". A better title might be \"Some basic metaphors from neuroscience that are useful in psycholinguistics\".","children":["**Activation** is how active a representation is.","**Spreading activation** passes activity over **weighted connections** to linked representations.","**Distributed representations** encode an item as a pattern across many units.","**Associative learning**, such as **Hebbian learning**, strengthens links between units that are active together.","**Bottom-up processing** runs from sensory features up to words, and **top-down processing** runs from word knowledge back down."]},{"text":"Together these ideas connect how knowledge is organized with the time course of recognizing and producing language.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p015":[{"text":"**A single neuron can respond most strongly to one specific combination of line orientation and movement.** **Hubel and Wiesel (1968)** related neural activity to sensory input, at the same time as the **Cognitive Revolution**.","children":["They recorded from **simple cells** in **primary visual cortex (V1)**.","A simple cell responds to lines of particular orientations in its **receptive field** (the part of the sensory input it responds to)."]},{"text":"They varied the orientation and movement of a line.","children":["Upward movement of a line at about **315 degrees** gave the **strongest response**.","Other orientations and movements gave much weaker responses."]},{"text":"The cell's **electrochemical activity** tells us about its representational and computational properties.","children":["The cell can be described as **representing** that combination of features."]}],"m1-lecture04-p016":[{"text":"**Cells further up the visual processing stream respond to complex patterns, such as another monkey's face in profile.**","children":["In data adapted from **Gross and colleagues (1985)**, a cell in **inferotemporal cortex** fires strongly for near-profile views of a monkey's face.","It fires much less for frontal and rear views.","Its responses are highest around 80 to 100 degrees of face angle."]},{"text":"The cell seems to **represent** something complex, not line segments.","children":["Its activation is **graded** (it can be a little active or very active)."]},{"text":"In psycholinguistics, **activation** extends this idea metaphorically.","children":["It applies to **perceptual representations**.","It applies to **conceptual representations**."]}],"m1-lecture04-p017":[{"text":"**A simple model treats each word as a node that a specific word can activate.**","children":["Imagine an incredibly simple set of **lexical representations** (stored representations of words).","Each one is a **node** (a unit with an activation value) in a network.","The network has three nodes, for lion, tiger and stripes."]},{"text":"The input and the nodes are different levels.","children":["The printed word LION supplies perceptual evidence.","The lion node is the word the system could recognize from that evidence."]}],"m1-lecture04-p018":[{"text":"**One word node has to respond to many printed forms of the same word.**","children":["LION, lion and Lion all name the same word.","Changes in typeface and size alter the display too.","All of them should activate the lion node."]},{"text":"This is the **lack of invariance problem** (one identity, many different sensory forms).","children":["The same problem came up with **phonemes**.","The **receptive fields** of word nodes would eventually have to be very complicated to handle it.","For now, imagine nodes that words can activate."]}],"m1-lecture04-p019":[{"text":"**A node can be only partly active when the input is weak.**","children":["LION appears inside a **noise mask** (visual noise around the word).","LION contrasts poorly with the mask.","So the input gives weak evidence.","So the lion node gets only **weak activation**."]},{"text":"Partial activation describes the state before recognition.","children":["The node can be partly active while recognition is still uncertain.","Its activation can rise as more evidence comes through."]}],"m1-lecture04-p020":[{"text":"**As the word becomes clearer, the activation of its node increases.**","children":["In the input, LION becomes easier to tell apart from the noise.","In the model, the lion node becomes **more active**."]},{"text":"Stronger evidence supports the same word more strongly.","children":["Recognition can be modeled as a response that grows with input strength.","Changing the display can change how a word is processed."]}],"m1-lecture04-p021":[{"text":"**When the signal is clear enough, activation is strong enough to count as identifying the word.**","children":["The **signal-to-noise ratio (SNR)** (the strength of the word signal relative to the noise) becomes very favorable.","The lion node becomes strongly active.","The simple network has **identified** lion.","Tiger and stripes are not identified."]},{"text":"This is the **perceptual identification task**.","children":["Participants push a button as soon as they can identify the item.","The time to identify it is the measure.","Easier conditions lead to an earlier response."]}],"m1-lecture04-p022":[{"text":"**Both the word itself and the experimental setting can change how quickly a word is identified.**","children":["**Features of the stimulus itself** concern the target word.","How often it is encountered is one.","How similar it is to other words is another."]},{"text":"**Features of the experimental environment** concern what surrounds the target.","children":["Having just seen the same word is one.","Having just seen a similar or related word is another."]},{"text":"The same target can be tested in different contexts.","children":["Different targets can be compared under the same task."]}],"m1-lecture04-p023":[{"text":"**Frequency, similarity to other words and recent exposure all change how quickly a word is identified.**","children":["**Word frequency** (how often a word is encountered) makes a word easier to activate.","Similarity to other words creates **neighborhood** relationships.","**Repetition priming** (faster identification after just seeing the same word) comes from recent exposure.","Just seeing a similar or related word can also prime it."]},{"text":"Frequency and repetition priming differ.","children":["Frequency reflects experience built up over time.","Repetition priming reflects a very recent presentation.","A related prime changes the context without repeating the target."]}],"m1-lecture04-p024":[{"text":"**Words that are encountered more often are easier to activate because experience tunes up their activation dynamics.**","children":["Most models of this kind assume that activation **unfolds over time**.","**Activation dynamics** (how activation rises and falls over time) are **tuned up by experience**.","Frequent words become more sensitive.","That is one way to explain **frequency effects**."]},{"text":"The **Interactive Activation model** of **Rumelhart and McClelland (1986)** simulates this kind of process.","children":["Activation for CAVE rises above competing words while the input supports it.","Then it gradually falls.","Different computational models simulate frequency effects in different ways."]}],"m1-lecture04-p025":[{"text":"**Seeing a word again soon makes it faster to identify because its activation has not fallen back yet.**","children":["Activation does not rise and fall instantly.","It may **fall off more slowly than it rises**.","Some activation remains after the word is gone."]},{"text":"The word appears again while activation is still high.","children":["Its **baseline** (starting activation level) is higher than usual.","So it can be activated more easily.","This is **repetition priming**, a basic effect in psychology."]}],"m1-lecture04-p026":[{"text":"**Neighborhood effects and related-word priming need connections between word nodes.**","children":["**Neighborhood effects** come from how similar a word is to other words.","Priming can come from a similar or related word.","That priming can be **associative**, **semantic** or **phonological**."]},{"text":"These two factors were left out so far.","children":["Isolated nodes can only change their own activity.","Repetition involves the same word twice.","These effects involve one word changing a different word."]}],"m1-lecture04-p027":[{"text":"**Many language phenomena require the nodes of the network to be connected.** After all, it is a network.","children":["Lion is connected to tiger.","Tiger is connected to stripes."]},{"text":"Activation can now arrive in two ways.","children":["The printed word activates lion **directly**.","A connection carries activity from lion to tiger **indirectly**.","A connection changes how one node responds when another is active."]}],"m1-lecture04-p028":[{"text":"**Activating one word sends some activation along weighted connections to associated words.** This is **spreading activation**.","children":["Connection strengths between nodes are set by their association, as in the norms.","Activating lion sends some activation along a **weighted connection** to tiger.","Different connections pass different amounts of activation."]},{"text":"This is a spreading activation account of **semantic priming** (faster processing of a word after a related word).","children":["Tiger is already partly active from lion.","So tiger is easier to recognize."]},{"text":"A semantic network links many concepts.","children":["Vehicle links to car and bus.","Fire engine links to the vehicles and to fire concepts."]}],"m1-lecture04-p029":[],"m1-lecture04-p030":[{"text":"**STRIPES starts out barely visible inside a strong noise mask.**","children":["This is the first display in a **perceptual identification** sequence.","The contrast is very low.","The letters are hard to separate from the background."]},{"text":"The target can begin to receive sensory support.","children":["The evidence is still too weak for a confident identification."]}],"m1-lecture04-p031":[{"text":"**As the contrast of STRIPES rises, more of its letter pattern stands out from the noise.**","children":["The letters are faint but easier to pick out.","The input gives **stronger support** for the word."]},{"text":"The word itself has not changed.","children":["Only the quality of the evidence has changed."]}],"m1-lecture04-p032":[{"text":"**STRIPES becomes more identifiable as its contrast improves.**","children":["The letters now stand apart from the noise.","The evidence for the word keeps growing."]},{"text":"The participant responds when the evidence is enough to recognize the word.","children":["Stimulus quality decides when that response becomes possible."]}],"m1-lecture04-p033":[{"text":"**The clearer STRIPES gives enough word information for the participant to respond.**","children":["The noise field is the same kind as before.","The letter pattern is clearer."]},{"text":"The participant can now make the **identification response**.","children":["The response reflects recognizing the word.","It is not a judgment about whether stripes go with a particular animal."]}],"m1-lecture04-p034":[{"text":"**At high contrast, STRIPES is easy to identify, and identifying it activates the stripes node.**","children":["The dark letters stand out from the gray noise.","Recognizing the word activates its node in the network."]},{"text":"The identified word can act as a **prime** (an earlier item that may change how a later item is processed).","children":["Its effect on another word depends on the connections between their nodes."]}],"m1-lecture04-p035":[{"text":"**LION is the target in a second identification sequence, and it starts out hard to read.**","children":["LION has weak contrast against the noise mask.","The letter pattern gives little usable information.","The short word sits in the center of the noise."]},{"text":"The target can be separated from its recent context.","children":["A **priming** account asks whether earlier experience changes how quickly LION is recognized."]}],"m1-lecture04-p036":[{"text":"**As the contrast of LION rises, the evidence for the word gets stronger.**","children":["LION is now faintly visible in the center.","The letters start to separate from the noise."]},{"text":"The same target produces a **changing internal response**.","children":["This happens because the quality of the sensory evidence changes.","Recognition becomes more likely."]}],"m1-lecture04-p037":[{"text":"**The time at which LION is identified is what a priming comparison measures.**","children":["LION is clearer inside the mask.","The four letters have more contrast than before.","A response can happen once the evidence supports recognizing the word."]},{"text":"A priming comparison asks whether recognition comes **sooner** after one preceding context than after another.","children":["LION is the common target in that comparison."]}],"m1-lecture04-p038":[{"text":"**LION is now legible, and both its clarity and any leftover activation from context help its node.**","children":["The noise mask is still present.","The stimulus gives substantial evidence for lion.","That evidence raises the activation of the lion node."]},{"text":"Two sources add to the lion node's state.","children":["One is the **clarity** of the target.","The other is **activation left by the context**."]}],"m1-lecture04-p039":[{"text":"**The clear LION display completes the sequence, and its node can be activated in two ways.**","children":["The strong word signal is easy to tell apart from the noise."]},{"text":"The two ways matter for the experiment.","children":["The printed word activates the lion node **directly**.","Other nodes can influence it **through connections**.","The experiment asks whether STRIPES helped LION through the network."]}],"m1-lecture04-p040":[{"text":"**Activating stripes primes lion even though the two words are not directly associated.** Activity flows throughout the network.","children":["Stripes is connected to tiger.","Tiger is connected to lion.","Activation from stripes reaches lion by way of tiger."]},{"text":"This is called **mediated priming** (priming that passes through a middle word, here tiger).","children":["A model with only direct associations has no route from stripes to lion.","So the effect is **compelling evidence for spreading activation**."]}],"m1-lecture04-p041":[{"text":"**The stripes and lion experiment is typical of psycholinguistics, with careful design and a small, counterintuitive effect.**","children":["It uses **careful study design**.","It finds **small effects**."]},{"text":"The result is **counterintuitive**. Why would stripes prime lion?","children":["Stripes and lion are not obvious direct associates.","Activity travels from stripes through tiger to lion.","A model with only direct associations cannot explain the effect.","So a small effect can tell the two accounts apart."]},{"text":"Three broader issues remain.","children":["They are top-down and bottom-up processing, learning and distributed representations."]}],"m1-lecture04-p042":[{"text":"**Word knowledge can feed back down and help complete a letter that is only partly visible.** **Bottom-up processing** (from visual features up to letters and words) and **top-down processing** (from an active word back down to its letters) work together.","children":["The **Interactive Activation network** after **McClelland and Rumelhart (1981)** has features at the bottom.","It has letters in the middle.","It has words such as WORK, WORD, WEAR and WEAK at the top."]},{"text":"In the example, blots cover part of the last letter of WORK.","children":["The visible features fit more than one letter.","WORK becomes active and sends activation back down to K.","The output for **K rises strongly** over time, while R and D stay low."]},{"text":"This is one way of modeling **pattern completion**, which Menn discusses at length.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture04-p043":[{"text":"**Associative learning strengthens connections between units that are active together.** **Hebbian learning** is summed up as \"neurons that fire together, wire together.\"","children":["The real process is more complicated.","The neural learning mechanisms that are relatively well understood are **associative**."]},{"text":"In the bell and food example, a bell signal and a food signal both feed a salivation unit.","children":["The bell and food inputs are active together.","The **synapse** from the bell is strengthened.","The bell then has more effect."]},{"text":"These mechanisms were once considered **insufficient** to learn anything important about language.","children":["Recent arguments ask what role they play.","They also ask how **innate knowledge** might constrain them.","**Large language models (LLMs)** learn many structural features of language with little more than this."]}],"m1-lecture04-p044":[{"text":"**Distributed representations let an associative model generalize what it learns to similar items.** A **distributed representation** (an item encoded as a pattern across many units) can capture similarity.","children":["**Generativity** is very important for language.","To generalize, a model must represent **similarity**.","Similar items have overlapping patterns."]},{"text":"What you know about robins and canaries can **generalize** to new birds as you learn about them.","children":[]},{"text":"The network takes an **item** input and a **relation** input.","children":["For canary with the relation can, the outputs include grow, move, fly and sing.","Hidden layers connect the item and relation to those properties."]}],"m1-lecture04-p045":[{"text":"**Five neurally inspired metaphors give psycholinguistics a vocabulary for how word knowledge works.**","children":["**Activation** changes as input is processed.","**Spreading activation** lets one representation influence another.","**Top-down and bottom-up processing** describe the direction of influence between levels.","**Associative learning**, such as Hebbian learning, changes connection strengths.","**Distributed representations** support similarity and generalization."]},{"text":"**Priming experiments** help reveal what people's **lexical networks** might look like.","children":["They measure how processing one item changes the processing of another."]}],"m1-lecture04-p046":[],"m1-lecture04-p047":[],"m1-lecture05-p001":[],"m1-lecture05-p002":[{"text":"**Phrenology tried to read brain functions from skull shape, and its chain of premises was only partly wrong.** A simplified phrenological chart assigns head regions to language, memory, thinking, love and other categories.","children":["Premise one says different parts of the brain do different processes.","Premise two says aptitude relates to brain region size.","Premise three says brain region size shapes the skull.","Careful skull measurements were then supposed to reveal brain functions."]},{"text":"No one believes in phrenology anymore, but its premises are not equally wrong.","children":["Premise one stays a serious idea, because brain parts can contribute differently to behavior.","Premise two needs a dependable link from ability to region size.","Premise three fails, because **skull shape is not a sensitive measure of brain shape**."]}],"m1-lecture05-p003":[{"text":"**Phrenology failed because it measured the wrong thing and defined functions too loosely.**","children":["The first problem was **inappropriate anatomical measurement** (using the skull to stand for the brain).","Skull shape is not a sensitive measure of brain shape."]},{"text":"The second problem was an **unconstrained definition of functions** (broad social labels treated as single mental functions).","children":["Studies of prisoners aimed to locate \"criminality\".","Studies of women with different numbers of children aimed to locate \"philopropogenitiveness\"."]},{"text":"A sound structure and function study needs both sides done well.","children":["It needs a valid anatomical measure.","It needs a clear account of the process being measured."]}],"m1-lecture05-p004":[{"text":"**Flourens attacked phrenology with lesion experiments and argued that lesion size mattered more than location.** He was an early and vociferous critic of phrenology.","children":["He used **experimental lesions** (damaging brain tissue and observing behavior).","He worked mainly with pigeons and rabbits."]},{"text":"His results had two parts.","children":["He found **distinct functions** for the cerebral cortex, the cerebellum and mid-brain structures.","He **failed to find** any match between specific cortical regions and specific senses."]},{"text":"He insisted that the **size of the lesion** was critical.","children":["When one sense was lost, all were lost together.","Removing most of the cortex makes sight, hearing and other senses disappear together."]}],"m1-lecture05-p005":[{"text":"**In 1861 Broca presented Tan, a patient who could not speak but seemed to understand.** Pierre Paul Broca was a neurologist studying **aphasia** (language loss after brain damage).","children":["Tan lost speech after a brain injury decades earlier.","Broca presented the case to the newly established Society of Anthropology.","Broca was also its founding president."]},{"text":"Tan's abilities came apart.","children":["Speaking was limited to **one jargon syllable**.","His senses seemed otherwise intact.","His **understanding** of what was said seemed preserved."]},{"text":"Tan was not the first observed case of a specific loss of speech.","children":["His case still suggested that speech production and comprehension can be damaged separately."]}],"m1-lecture05-p006":[{"text":"**Tan's lesion gave the name Broca's area to part of the left frontal lobe, but finding damage is not finding function.**","children":["Tan's brain is on display at the Musee Dupuytren.","The lesion was **large**.","It definitely included the **inferior frontal gyrus** (the third convolution of the frontal lobe)."]},{"text":"**Broca's area** is a portion of the **left frontal lobe** important for speech.","children":[]},{"text":"**Hughlings Jackson (1874)** objected at the time.","children":["He wrote that \"to locate the damage which destroys speech and to locate speech are two different things\".","Damage in one spot can stop speech.","That does not show where speech itself is produced."]}],"m1-lecture05-p007":[{"text":"**Lichtheim and Wernicke explained different aphasias as disconnections between language centers.** In the 1870s these German neurologists made more systematic observations of aphasia.","children":["A **center** is a proposed brain location for one function.","A **disconnection** is a break in the pathway between two centers."]},{"text":"In Lichtheim's house model, each letter is a center.","children":["A stands for sound images.","B stands for concepts.","M stands for movement images."]},{"text":"Each loss maps to a broken link.","children":["A loss of understanding spoken words may come from an A to B disconnection.","A loss of verbatim repetition may come from an A to M disconnection."]},{"text":"Describing centers for specific functions is an ancestor of **modularity**.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture05-p008":[{"text":"**Freud doubted that the brain is organized by our psychological categories.** He critiqued the \"diagram-drawing\" school of aphasia research in his early neurological work.","children":["He felt it was naive to expect brain areas for \"word forms\" or \"concepts\".","He proposed that language emerges from **\"fields\"**.","These fields connect perceptual and motor abilities to **\"drives\"**."]},{"text":"This is a **holist critique** (language spread over connected systems, not set in centers).","children":[]},{"text":"It raises a durable tension in cognitive neuroscience.","children":["Can brain area functions be related directly to cognitive functions?","Is the brain really organized according to our descriptions of cognition?"]}],"m1-lecture05-p009":[{"text":"**MRI showed that Broca's original lesion was much deeper than Broca's area.** **Dronkers and colleagues** used MRI to assess damage in Broca's landmark cases very precisely, including Leborgne's brain.","children":["The lesion is very deep.","It includes the **insula**.","It damages much white matter in the **superior longitudinal fasciculus** (a tract connecting the front and back of the cortex)."]},{"text":"Other work by this group points away from Broca's area.","children":["The **insula**, not Broca's area, is strongly associated with **speech apraxia**.","Speech apraxia is the condition Broca described in his patients."]}],"m1-lecture05-p010":[{"text":"**Broca's description of behavior and his anatomy were both crude because of the methods of his time.**","children":["Broca is still discussed 150 years later despite these limits."]},{"text":"His **behavioral** description was crude.","children":["Linguistics then focused on historical issues, such as how languages were related.","Linguists also worked on reconstructing ancient languages.","Experimental psychology had not really been invented yet.","Observations of behavior were necessarily informal."]},{"text":"His **anatomical** localization was crude and probably wrong.","children":["Fine-grained descriptions of the cortex were still in their infancy.","**Brodmann's map** was not published until 1909."]}],"m1-lecture05-p011":[{"text":"**Broca's presentation of Tan made linking functions to brain regions a respectable pursuit.** Other researchers showed similar things around the same time.","children":["**The Daxes** reported related findings.","**Wernicke** reported a patient who was **fluent** but produced **meaningless gibberish**.","Wernicke's data are, if anything, more interesting because of that contrast with Tan."]},{"text":"For a variety of reasons, Broca's case is still seen as a **watershed moment** in neuropsychology.","children":["It turned relating specific functions to specific brain regions into a serious research question."]}],"m1-lecture05-p012":[],"m1-lecture05-p013":[{"text":"**Cognitive neuropsychology needs cognitive psychology to define the functions it relates to brain damage.**","children":["**Cognitive neuropsychology** (the study of how brain damage changes mental functions) supplied much 20th century knowledge about structure and function.","It relied on experimental observations of brain-damaged individuals."]},{"text":"Cognitive theory proposes which operations might be separable.","children":["One question asks if recognizing **abstract word forms** separates from computing **meaning**.","**Physical identity** matching compares letters by appearance.","**Name identity** matching compares letters by name.","**Rule identity** matching sorts letters as vowels or consonants."]},{"text":"**Quantitative measurements** test such questions.","children":["They allow **statistical comparison**.","This is critical because **deficits are rarely complete**."]}],"m1-lecture05-p014":[{"text":"**Anatomical imaging locates injuries in living patients, but it still shows damage, not normal function.**","children":["Scientists no longer wait for patients to \"come to autopsy\" to find a lesion.","A scan of **H.M.'s** middle temporal lobes is one example.","His damage resulted from surgery."]},{"text":"The warning from **Hughlings Jackson (1874)** still holds.","children":["Locating the damage that destroys speech differs from locating speech.","Patient studies show behavior **after** a region is damaged.","They tell only **indirectly** what the intact region does."]}],"m1-lecture05-p015":[{"text":"**PET and fMRI measure brains in action only indirectly, slowly and coarsely.** **Posner and colleagues** described **PET** studies that used radio-labeled oxygen as an activity marker.","children":["These methods are **metabolic** (they track an index of oxygen consumption, not neural activity).","They are **slow**, since metabolic processes unfold over seconds.","Important neural events can occur in milliseconds or less.","They have only **fair spatial resolution**, on the order of millimeters.","Important events happen at the scale of microns.","FMRI has better resolution than PET."]}],"m1-lecture05-p016":[{"text":"**Imaging needs a control condition because the whole brain is active all the time.**","children":["A claim that \"x part of the brain is active during y task\" cannot stand alone.","Activity occurs all over the brain all of the time."]},{"text":"**Subtraction logic** (task image minus control image) isolates the difference.","children":["The control shares most demands with the task.","A motor image minus a rest image leaves a much smaller region."]},{"text":"**Posner and colleagues** applied this logic to language.","children":["Their tasks shared visual input and responses.","The tasks differed in whether **word meaning** had to be accessed.","Subtracting the simpler task isolates semantic processing from word and letter form identification."]}],"m1-lecture05-p017":[{"text":"**Semantic tasks activated a left inferior frontal region that roughly matches Broca's area.** **Posner and colleagues** compared three meaning tasks with simpler tasks.","children":["Monitoring words for dangerous animals was compared with passively viewing words.","Generating uses for written words was compared with repeating them.","Generating uses for heard words was compared with repeating them."]},{"text":"All three contrasts activated the **left inferior frontal** region.","children":["The activity appeared for both seen and heard words.","It links the region to **semantic processing** of words.","The region corresponds **roughly to Broca's area**."]}],"m1-lecture05-p018":[{"text":"**Imaging can be better than phrenology, but only when its measures and definitions are sound.** The question \"Is this better than phrenology?\" is rhetorical but worth exploring.","children":[]},{"text":"Phrenology failed on two points.","children":["Its measure, skull shape, was invalid.","Its functions, such as \"criminality\", were undefined."]},{"text":"Imaging improves both points in part.","children":["It measures an index of brain activity.","It defines operations through **controlled task contrasts**."]},{"text":"An image of an active region still proves nothing alone.","children":["The contrast must isolate the proposed operation.","The measurement must support linking that operation to the region."]}],"m1-lecture05-p019":[{"text":"**Posner and colleagues localized component operations, not whole tasks.**","children":["The operations work on **visual**, **phonological** and **semantic codes**."]},{"text":"Whole tasks involve many coordinated computations.","children":["Reading a word is one such task.","Forming a visual image is another.","Shifting attention is another.","No whole task is assigned to one brain area."]},{"text":"The PET evidence **complemented lesion evidence**.","children":["It linked cognitive distinctions to activity during performance.","Averaged blood-flow patterns suggested **consistency** across participants.","The participants were right-handed and good readers."]}],"m1-lecture05-p020":[{"text":"**Language suits localization research because it splits into separable, testable abilities.** Language has turned up at key moments in debates about localization of function.","children":["**Broca's presentation of Tan** helped establish **lesion-behavior analysis**.","Some first **PET** and **fMRI** studies used activation of **Broca's area** in language tasks.","Those studies treated it as evidence that cognitive functions could be localized."]},{"text":"Language abilities come apart in clear ways.","children":["Tan lost speaking but seemed to keep understanding.","Understanding, repeating and judging meaning place different demands on processing.","These differences give clear contrasts for lesion studies and control conditions."]}],"m1-lecture05-p021":[],"m1-lecture06-p001":[],"m1-lecture06-p002":[{"text":"**The Cognitive Revolution brought internal mental states back into explanations of language.** There is a very old tradition of describing languages in abstract, formal terms like those used in Artificial Intelligence.","children":["A **formal symbolic system** (abstract symbols plus rules) is one way to describe a language.","Experimental psychology in the 1950s and 1960s was actively hostile to talk of an **internal state**.","Behaviorism explained learning through observable stimuli, responses and consequences instead."]},{"text":"Two works framed the debate.","children":["**Skinner's Verbal Behavior** tried to explain language learning by **operant conditioning** (learning through reinforced responses).","**Chomsky's (1959) review** of Verbal Behavior challenged that account.","The review is better remembered than the book itself.","It was an important moment in **the Cognitive Revolution**."]},{"text":"For Chomsky, a **grammar** is an internal system.","children":["It characterizes sentences a person has never encountered."]}],"m1-lecture06-p003":[{"text":"**The formal tradition grew out of early computers, which raised the question of how machines differ from human intelligence.** Interest in **Artificial Intelligence** dates back to the beginning of the digital computer.","children":["**Alan Turing** thought about this question a lot.","He developed decryption devices for the British Army during World War II."]},{"text":"Computers were invented as **universal function approximators** (machines that carry out many kinds of calculations and logical operations).","children":["No machine had done so many different kinds of operations before.","Earlier machines did one fixed task.","This flexibility raised the question of how the computer differed from human intelligence, if at all."]},{"text":"The question links language to AI.","children":["If language is symbols and procedures, a computer can explore what those procedures accomplish."]}],"m1-lecture06-p004":[{"text":"**The Turing test counts a computer as intelligent if it can replace a player in the imitation game without the examiner noticing.** The **imitation game** has three players.","children":["A and B are a man and a woman.","C is an examiner whose job is to figure out the gender of A and B.","A tries to fool C.","B tries to help C guess correctly."]},{"text":"In the test, a **computer** replaces either A or B.","children":["If C does not notice, the computer has demonstrated intelligence.","C never inspects the program.","C judges only the conversation."]},{"text":"The test turns intelligence into **observable conversational performance**.","children":["**Natural language** is the medium of the judgment."]}],"m1-lecture06-p005":[{"text":"**Turing's proposal centers on ordinary language use and on learning, not on expert skills.** The test has several interesting features.","children":["It depends on **natural language**.","It involves **deception**, because one player tries to fool the examiner.","Turing explicitly excluded **expert behaviors**, such as calculating large sums or composing poetry."]},{"text":"Turing also concluded how best to build an intelligent machine.","children":["Build something with the cognitive capabilities of a **child**.","Let it learn to be more like an **adult**.","Intelligence then develops through experience.","It is not supplied as a finished list of adult answers."]}],"m1-lecture06-p006":[{"text":"**Newell and Simon's General Problem Solver solved formal problems by applying procedures to symbols.** It is a later example of **GOFAI** (the approach that models intelligence as operations on explicit symbols).","children":["It takes an abstract description of a formal problem.","It applies procedures to symbols to find a solution.","Each configuration is a **state**, and a legal move leads to another state.","A solution is a path of legal moves to the **goal state**."]},{"text":"It was very good at games and puzzles.","children":["The **Tower of Hanoi** is one example. Each arrangement of disks is a state.","**Chess** is another example."]},{"text":"It was also good at mathematics and logic.","children":["It handled **geometry proofs**.","It handled **algebra word problems**."]}],"m1-lecture06-p007":[{"text":"**Early AI favored formal problems because of limited technology and because of a belief about what intelligence is.** Two reasons drove the emphasis.","children":[]},{"text":"The first reason was **technology**.","children":["Inputs were still very limited, such as punchcards and machine language.","No one could input a high-resolution image and ask the computer to find the face."]},{"text":"The second reason was **theoretical commitments**.","children":["Intelligence was thought to be exemplified by **rational behavior** of this kind.","You can train a **pigeon** to guide a missile, but it does not know what it is doing.","Whether AI programs know what they are doing is a deep argument too.","AI programs at least **represent goal states** and choose procedures for achieving them."]}],"m1-lecture06-p008":[{"text":"**Chomsky argued that knowing a language means having an internalized grammar, not a set of conditioned responses.** A **grammar** (a system that enumerates sentences and gives each a structural description) captures remarkable abilities.","children":["Speakers tell **sentences from nonsentences**.","Speakers understand **new sentences**, in part.","Speakers notice **ambiguities**."]},{"text":"New sentences are not related to familiar ones by any simple **similarity**.","children":["The similarity is not formal, semantic, statistical or a shared grammatical frame.","So Chomsky called talk of **generalization** pointless and empty.","We recognize a new sentence because our grammar **generates** it.","We understand it partly by working out how the grammar **derives** it."]},{"text":"All normal children acquire comparable complex grammars with remarkable speed.","children":["Chomsky took this to mean humans are **specially designed** for it.","He proposed a **hypothesis-formulating** ability of unknown character."]}],"m1-lecture06-p009":[{"text":"**Linguistic knowledge is a grammar, so people can judge whether any new sentence is grammatical.** A **grammar** is a set of abstract rules that produce grammatical sentences.","children":["The number of grammatical sentences is **infinite**.","People still make a **grammaticality judgment** (a decision about whether a sentence is well formed) for any new sentence.","That ability is remarkable because the sentence was never heard."]},{"text":"The grammar also handles ambiguity.","children":["One string of words can get more than one structure.","So the words alone do not fix the meaning."]},{"text":"These judgments become the main evidence.","children":["People's **intuitions** about grammaticality are the key data for the study of language."]}],"m1-lecture06-p010":[{"text":"**Grammatical sentences share no obvious similarity, so stimulus generalization cannot explain how people handle new ones.** **Stimulus generalization** (a trained response spreading to similar stimuli) is the behaviorist account of novelty.","children":["It needs a similarity between old and new items.","Grammatical sentences have no obvious similarities to one another.","A grammar can generate an infinite number of sentences.","Generalization cannot cover that infinite set."]},{"text":"Chomsky offered another account.","children":["We recognize a new sentence because our **internalized grammar** generates it.","It does not have to match a familiar sentence.","Spreading a trained response and recognizing an abstract grammatical relation are different processes."]}],"m1-lecture06-p011":[{"text":"**Conditioned responses generalize along concrete dimensions like color, and it is unclear how that reaches abstract concepts.** A **pigeon** trained to peck for a **green light** also pecks at similar colors.","children":["Pecking is strongest near green.","Pecking falls as the color becomes less similar.","This pattern is a **generalization gradient** (response strength falling with distance from the trained stimulus)."]},{"text":"Another pigeon does a **magnitude discrimination** task.","children":["It responds according to amounts.","Number is another concrete dimension."]},{"text":"Generalization in operant conditioning depends on concrete similarity.","children":["Examples are **color** and **number**.","Grammar has no concrete dimension like these.","So it is not clear how conditioning extends to **abstract concepts**."]}],"m1-lecture06-p012":[{"text":"**Stimulus control explains an utterance only if the controlling stimulus can be named apart from the utterance itself.** **Stimulus control** is Skinner's idea that an aspect of the environment controls a response.","children":["Skinner's typical example is a response to a piece of **music**.","The music is the stimulus.","The utterance is the response."]},{"text":"Chomsky said other examples only add to the **general mystification**.","children":["Skinner treated a **proper noun** as a response to a stimulus."]},{"text":"The trouble is finding the stimulus.","children":["A piece of music has many properties.","A speaker can respond to any of them.","If the utterance picks out the stimulus, the utterance ends up deciding its own cause."]}],"m1-lecture06-p013":[{"text":"**Skinner posed the wrong question, because what people say depends on far more than the stimuli around them.** Chomsky's point is that we do not know the current stimuli until the speaker responds.","children":["So stimulus control cannot predict speech.","Naming the stimulus after the response makes the account **circular**."]},{"text":"What a person says depends on many factors.","children":["**Social interactions** matter.","**Cognitive processes** matter.","**Previous experiences** matter.","Much of this has nothing to do with language."]},{"text":"The **tractable problem** (one that can actually be solved) is different.","children":["Study the **structure** of what people say.","Derive the set of **rules** that produces that structure."]}],"m1-lecture06-p014":[{"text":"**A sentence can be grammatical even when its meaning makes little literal sense, and that judgment does not come from having heard it.** Chomsky (1955) compared two sentences. Neither sentence, nor any of their parts, had occurred in an English speaker's experience.","children":["Sentence (a) is \"colorless green ideas sleep furiously\".","Sentence (b) is \"furiously sleep ideas green colorless\".","Sentence (a) is **grammatical**.","Sentence (b) is **not grammatical**."]},{"text":"The two sentences use the same words.","children":["In (a), adjectives come before the noun, followed by a verb and adverb.","In (b), the order breaks that structure.","So the difference lies in **structure**, not in familiarity."]},{"text":"The reverse case also holds.","children":["The warning \"Car! Getting towed! Yours!\" is **ungrammatical**.","Yet the stimulus that likely controlled it is perfectly clear."]}],"m1-lecture06-p015":[{"text":"**Chomsky describes language at Marr's computational level of analysis.** **David Marr** was a visual neuroscientist.","children":["He said cognitive systems have three **levels of analysis**."]},{"text":"The three levels answer different questions.","children":["The **computational level** states what problem the system solves.","The **algorithmic level** states which representations and procedures solve it.","The **implementational level** states how the procedures are physically carried out."]},{"text":"A claim can be useful at one level without answering the others.","children":["Chomsky's abstract grammar is a computational description.","It states what language processing must achieve."]}],"m1-lecture06-p016":[{"text":"**A cash register performs the same function no matter what machine carries it out.** This example borrows heavily from **Sharon Goldwater**.","children":[]},{"text":"Each level gives a different description.","children":["At the **computational level**, it takes item prices and determines the amount due. Given the payment, it computes the change.","At the **algorithmic level**, it uses addition for the price and subtraction for the change.","At the **implementational level**, the machinery does not matter for these functions."]},{"text":"Many devices count as cash registers.","children":["A mechanical register works.","A credit card reader works.","A computer program works.","For the same prices and payment, each gives the same change."]}],"m1-lecture06-p017":[{"text":"**Treating language as a Type I problem lets researchers study grammar while ignoring how it is implemented.** **Type I problems** (Marr's term) break down cleanly into his levels.","children":["The computational level for language is **Universal Grammar**.","The algorithmic level covers specific processes and how they apply in different languages.","The implementational level can be set aside."]},{"text":"Setting aside implementation ignores several factors.","children":["**Errors** are ignored.","**Memory limitations** are ignored.","**Associative learning** is ignored."]},{"text":"The chronology matters.","children":["Chomsky's critique came about a decade before Marr's theoretical work.","Both were influential in AI and in **GOFAI** theory."]}],"m1-lecture06-p018":[{"text":"**A Type 2 problem is solved by many interacting processes and cannot be split into a clean computational theory.** Marr linked his computational level to Chomsky.","children":["Chomsky's **competence theory** for English syntax is what Marr meant by a computational theory.","Both care little about the details of algorithms.","One must know what algorithms should do before devising them.","A problem that divides this way has a **Type 1 theory**."]},{"text":"Many problems of biological information processing have a Type 1 theory.","children":["There is no reason they all should."]},{"text":"Some problems have a **Type 2 theory**.","children":["Many processes act at the same time.","Their interaction is its own **simplest description**."]}],"m1-lecture06-p019":[{"text":"**Marr suggested that the grammar of natural language may have a Type 2 theory rather than a Type 1 theory.** Marr described what language does.","children":["A speaker holds a **data structure** that is not one-dimensional.","Language turns it into a **sequential utterance** (a one-dimensional form).","The listener rebuilds a rough copy in their head."]},{"text":"This changes how syntax might be explained.","children":["There may be no Type 1 theory of English syntax of the kind **transformational grammar** tries to define.","Its constraints may be **wired-in conventions** for doing this tedious but vital operation.","They may not be deep principles about intelligence."]},{"text":"An abstract theory of syntax may be an **illusion**.","children":["It may only approximate the real processes.","**Julesz's second order statistics theory** approximates texture vision in the same way."]}],"m1-lecture06-p020":[{"text":"**How language is implemented in the brain might be unavoidable in a theory of language.** If language comes from interacting processes, those processes belong in the theory.","children":["Learning then matters to the theory.","Memory then matters too.","Perception and action matter as well."]},{"text":"A whole alternative approach to language research rests on this hypothesis.","children":["The first approach starts with an abstract grammar for the brain to carry out.","The alternative starts with the learner and the processing system.","It asks what language-like organization can develop from them."]}],"m1-lecture06-p021":[{"text":"**Emergentist approaches make their strongest claims at the implementational level.** The approach is also called **statistical learning** or **parallel distributed processing**.","children":[]},{"text":"Its commitments are specific.","children":["It uses **distributed representations** (non-hierarchical activity spread over many units). These are like activity patterns over groups of neurons.","It uses **gradual learning** (slowly adjusting connection strengths). This is like adjusting the strength of synaptic connections.","It focuses on **microstructure** (the many small interactions that produce a larger regularity).","It draws a more **graded division** between levels of analysis."]},{"text":"The learning mechanism is expected to shape the apparent rules in behavior.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture06-p022":[{"text":"**Elman (1990) built simple recurrent networks that learn language patterns only by predicting the next item.** **Simple recurrent networks** capture different aspects of language performance in this framework.","children":["All the model does is predict the next item.","It gradually learns to do this with experience."]},{"text":"Information flows through four groups of units.","children":["The **input** feeds the **hidden units**.","The hidden units feed the **output** prediction.","A copy of hidden activity goes to **context units**.","The context units feed back into the hidden units with the next input."]},{"text":"Some connections learn and one does not.","children":["Trainable connections adjust to improve prediction.","The copying connection is fixed.","So the prediction depends on the current item and what came before."]}],"m1-lecture06-p023":[{"text":"**A letter-prediction network finds word boundaries without ever being given spaces.** The model receives \"Manyyearsagoaboyandgirllivedbytheseatheyplayedhappily\".","children":["That string becomes \"many years ago a boy and girl lived by the sea they played happily\"."]},{"text":"The model only predicts the next letter.","children":["**Prediction error** (how far the guess is from the real next letter) is tracked letter by letter."]},{"text":"The error follows the words.","children":["At the start of a word, error is high.","Within a word, error goes down.","At the next word, error rises again.","So the error peaks mark the **word boundaries**."]}],"m1-lecture06-p024":[{"text":"**A network trained only to predict words in simple sentences ends up grouping words by grammar and meaning.** The categories are a **side effect** of learning to predict sequences.","children":["No one labels the words.","Elman examined the **hidden-unit activation patterns** for each word.","Words used in similar contexts got similar patterns."]},{"text":"The patterns cluster by **grammatical class**.","children":["**Nouns** form one group.","**Verbs** form another."]},{"text":"The patterns also carry **semantic information**.","children":["Nouns split into animates and inanimates.","Animates split into humans and animals.","Inanimates include food and breakable things.","Verbs split by how they take a **direct object**."]}],"m1-lecture06-p025":[{"text":"**A statistical model trained on text can tell \"colorless green ideas sleep furiously\" from its reversed form.** Many engineering solutions in **Natural Language Processing** work this way.","children":["They analyze **massive corpora** (collections of texts and transcriptions of spoken language).","They find statistical patterns in them."]},{"text":"**Peter Norvig** discussed Chomsky's critique.","children":["He agreed neither sentence appeared in the published literature before 1955.","But every two-word part had occurred.","\"Colorless green\" appeared in 1942.","\"Green ideas\" appeared in 1922.","\"Ideas sleep in books\" appeared in 1912."]},{"text":"**Pereira (2001)** tested a **finite-state model** with word categories.","children":["It was trained by **expectation maximization** on newspaper text.","It computed that sentence (a) is **200,000 times more probable** than sentence (b)."]}],"m1-lecture06-p026":[],"m1-lecture06-p027":[{"text":"**Psycholinguistics has two traditions that disagree about what linguistic knowledge is.** The first is the **GOFAI, modular or Chomskyan** tradition.","children":["Linguistic knowledge is essentially abstract and symbolic.","Linguistic representations differ in kind from other knowledge.","They must interface with messy perceptual, motor, memory and other cognitive capacities.","The psycholinguist studies this **interface**."]},{"text":"The second is the **emergentist or functionalist** tradition.","children":["Symbol and rule-like behavior are **emergent properties** of statistical learning.","Perceptual, motor and cognitive processes actually shape linguistic representations.","The psycholinguist studies **language use** and relates it to general psychological phenomena."]}],"m1-lecture06-p028":[],"m1-lecture06-p029":[],"m1-lecture06-p030":[{"text":"**Messiness like the exceptions to the \"i before e\" rule appears at every level of every language.** This messiness motivates the **emergentist or statistical learning** approach.","children":["**Phonetic categories** are defined probabilistically over multiple dimensions.","**Words** are ambiguous in every way imaginable.","**Morphological paradigms** have exceptions. \"Add -ed\" for the English past tense fails for RUN-RAN and SLEEP-SLEPT.","**Syntactic rules** vary across dialects and individuals and change over time."]},{"text":"A strong pattern and its exceptions coexist.","children":["Adding -ed works for many verbs.","Irregular forms stay part of what speakers know."]}],"m1-lecture06-p031":[{"text":"**Moravec's paradox says tasks that are easy for computers are hard for humans, and the reverse.** This is a problem for the **GOFAI** approach.","children":["For computers, proving theorems and solving geometry problems is comparatively easy.","For computers, recognizing a face is extremely difficult.","For computers, crossing a room without bumping into anything is extremely difficult.","For humans, the opposite is true."]},{"text":"The paradox raises a question about language.","children":["Characterizing language in formal terms, as for a computer program, may be a **false trail**."]}],"m1-lecture06-p032":[{"text":"**Fixed categories and rules are often fuzzy in practice, and this has been known for a long time.** **Wittgenstein (1953)** made the point in **Philosophical Investigations**.","children":["His example is the category of **games**.","Board games, card games, ball games and Olympic games are all games.","No single feature belongs to every one."]},{"text":"The members are linked by **family resemblance** (overlapping similarities with no one shared feature).","children":["One pair shares one feature.","Another pair shares a different feature.","A category does not need one strict list of necessary properties."]}],"m1-lecture06-p033":[{"text":"**Many phenomena in language are probabilistic, just as games share overlapping features rather than one definition.** **Wittgenstein (1953)** compared many games.","children":["Chess and noughts and crosses are not amusing in the same way.","Patience has no competition between players.","Ball games have winning and losing, but a child throwing a ball at a wall does not.","Skill in chess differs from skill in tennis.","Ring-a-ring-a-roses keeps amusement but loses many other features."]},{"text":"Similarities crop up and disappear from game to game.","children":["They form a complicated network, **overlapping and criss-crossing**.","Some are overall similarities.","Some are similarities of detail."]}],"m1-lecture06-p034":[{"text":"**Psychologists produced results that made a lot of cognition seem less suited to formal, rational interpretation.** Their work stressed **family resemblances**, **similarity-based generalization** and **hunches**.","children":["**Roger Shepard** worked on **perception**.","**Eleanor Rosch** worked on **categorization**.","**Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky** worked on **decision-making**.","Many others did similar work."]},{"text":"Their results pointed away from a purely **formal, rational** account of the mind.","children":["This supports asking whether language also reflects similarity-based generalization."]}],"m1-lecture07-p001":[],"m1-lecture07-p002":[{"text":"**Speech errors are linguistic evidence because the form of each error reveals how an utterance was planned.**","children":["An error can split a word into parts.","An error can move a sound into another position.","An error can replace the intended word with a related one."]},{"text":"People make speech errors because many items are active at once.","children":["**Noise**, **frequency**, other active material and **similarity** can each let the wrong item win."]},{"text":"**Fromkin**'s title \"Speech errors as linguistic evidence\" is gutsy.","children":["If **competence** (knowledge of the language) is studied apart from **performance** (actual speaking), an error is just something to set aside.","Fromkin instead treats the structure of errors as evidence about the language system.","Comparing the error with the intended form reveals which parts changed and which stayed."]}],"m1-lecture07-p003":[{"text":"**In spreading activation models, an error happens when an unintended item gets enough activation to beat the intended one.** Four key concepts explain where that activation comes from.","children":["**Noise** (fluctuation and interference in activation) is not only literal acoustic noise, though that can cause problems too.","**Frequency** matters because common words are easier to activate than less common ones. This seems to affect many levels of speech production.","**Other sources of activation** include what was just said and what is planned next. Thoughts the speaker is trying not to say may add activation too.","**Similarity** lets activation spread more easily between related items. It can be semantic, syntactic or phonological."]},{"text":"These sources can combine on one competitor.","children":["That is how an unintended candidate can enter the utterance."]}],"m1-lecture07-p004":[{"text":"**A tip-of-the-tongue state is partial activation of a word's meaning and sound that falls short of the threshold for saying it.** The person knows the meaning but cannot produce the word.","children":["Less frequent words are most susceptible, because their forms are harder to activate.","A **prime** (a related word read first) can help, because it adds activation to the missing sound form."]},{"text":"**Lise Abrams** reviewed an example with the target \"contraband\" (goods traded illegally).","children":["People in a **tip-of-the-tongue (TOT) state** read a prime and then tried the target again.","First-syllable primes such as \"contact,\" \"connotation\" and \"conscious\" resolved about **50 percent** of TOT states.","Middle-syllable primes gave intermediate resolution.","Final-syllable primes were close to the unrelated condition.","Unrelated words resolved only about **one quarter**."]},{"text":"Sound overlap at the start of the word helps recovery most.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p005":[{"text":"**Slips of the tongue are classified by which direction a sound moves between the intended and the produced form.** Spooner supposedly once said \"Mardon me padam, this pie is occupewed. Can I sew you to another sheet?\"","children":["These examples come from Fromkin."]},{"text":"The three types answer the same question, which is where the misplaced sound came from.","children":["**Anticipation** (a later sound comes too soon) turns \"week long race\" into \"reek long race.\"","**Perseveration** (an earlier sound carries forward) turns \"John gave the boy\" into \"John gave the goy.\"","**Metathesis** (an exchange, where two sounds trade places) turns \"keep a tape\" into \"teep a cape.\" The k and t swap, and the rest stays put."]},{"text":"The type depends on the relation between the two forms.","children":["Whether the result makes sense does not matter."]}],"m1-lecture07-p006":[{"text":"**Other sources of activation are the spreading activation concept most obviously at work in these slips.** Material just said and material planned next are both active while a person talks.","children":["In anticipation, planned material is active too early, so the r in \"reek long race\" arrives before its place.","In perseveration, spoken material stays active, so the g in \"John gave the goy\" lingers after its place.","In an exchange, two elements in the same plan interfere with each other's positions."]},{"text":"The other concepts still play a part.","children":["**Similarity** and **frequency** decide which candidates compete well.","**Noise** can decide which candidate gets picked."]},{"text":"Planning reaches past the sound being said right now.","children":["This lets the speaker prepare what comes next.","It also lets the wrong active element fill a position."]}],"m1-lecture07-p007":[{"text":"**A mondegreen is a slip of the ear, in which a listener groups the speech sounds into different words than the speaker meant.**","children":["The term comes from hearing \"laid him on the green\" as \"Lady Mondegreen.\"","\"Hold me closer, tiny dancer\" gets misheard as \"Hold me closer, Tony Danza.\" It is \"Tiny Dancer,\" like the song title.","**Andrew Nevins** is analyzing crowdsourced mondegreens from kissthisguy.com."]},{"text":"Speech is ambiguous because it arrives as a continuous stream.","children":["Listeners have to infer the **word boundaries** (where one word ends and the next begins).","\"The good candy came anyways\" and \"The good can decay many ways\" sound nearly alike.","**Context** is what tells them apart."]},{"text":"Context matters most in songs.","children":["In songs, **prosody** (the rhythm and melody of speech) is less helpful.","So a familiar name or phrase can take over."]}],"m1-lecture07-p008":[{"text":"**A malapropism like this is a perceptual error, because the speaker says exactly what they intend to say.**","children":["The speaker once heard a word like \"congenital\" (present from birth).","The speaker decided the intended word must have been a more familiar one, like \"congenial.\"","The speaker now says \"a congenial heart defect\" and means it."]},{"text":"This is **top-down processing** (known words and expectations shaping what is heard).","children":["A familiar word helps organize the heard sound.","The familiar word wins over the less familiar target.","Other examples include \"lettuce days,\" \"decaffeinated him\" and \"Utopian tubes tied.\""]},{"text":"A production slip and a mislearned expression can sound equally odd.","children":["They come from different processes."]}],"m1-lecture07-p009":[{"text":"**Fromkin's errors break up consonant clusters, so single segments are units in speech planning.** At the time, people argued about whether the **phoneme** (or phone) was a planning unit.","children":["If whole **clusters** always moved together, errors would look like \"gish frotto\" or \"clesh freer water.\"","Fromkin presents evidence that clusters get broken up all the time instead."]},{"text":"Each error moves only one sound of a cluster.","children":["\"Fish grotto\" became \"frish gotto.\" The r left gr and joined the f.","\"Fresh clear water\" became \"flesh queer water.\" The r and l exchanged, and f and k stayed put.","\"Split pea soup\" became \"plit spea soup.\" Only the s moved."]},{"text":"A model therefore needs access to each individual segment.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture07-p010":[{"text":"**Fromkin's 1971 model traces how a meaning to be conveyed becomes an utterance through a fixed series of stages.** It is an early version of the model Menn describes.","children":["First, syntactic and semantic generators build **syntactic-semantic structures**.","Next, an intonation contour generator adds **primary stress** and **intonation**.","Then the **lexicon** supplies words specified for features and order of segments.","This gives strings of segments divided into syllables.","**Morphophonemic rules** and **phonetic rules** fully specify the sounds.","Last, **motor commands** to the muscles produce the **utterance**."]},{"text":"Each stage is a separate representation.","children":["So an error can hit one stage while the others stay organized.","Different errors give evidence about different stages."]}],"m1-lecture07-p011":[{"text":"**The basic model of speech production has five levels, from the message down to the speech gesture.**","children":["The **message level** holds what the speaker intends to convey.","The **functional level** picks **lemmas** (word identities before their final form) and assigns **semantic roles**.","The **positional level** arranges them into a clause.","The **phonological encoding level** specifies the sounds.","The **speech gesture level** prepares the movements."]},{"text":"Following Menn, the first two levels come first.","children":["The sound slips were already phonological encoding errors."]},{"text":"The levels separate things the everyday word \"word\" blurs together.","children":["Choosing a fitting word is one job.","Choosing its grammatical form is another job.","Assembling its sounds is a third job."]}],"m1-lecture07-p012":[{"text":"**The message level decides what information a speaker wants to convey, and the same event can become different messages.** Take \"I won a bunch of money on the game Sunday.\"","children":["With a fan of the losing team, the listener's feelings may change how directly the win is stressed.","With someone not interested in football, the speaker may need to explain why it matters.","In a tweet, the short public post changes which details get included."]},{"text":"The event stays the same, but purpose and chosen details change.","children":[]},{"text":"The message level is mysterious and hard to investigate experimentally.","children":["Giving everyone the same event does not mean they all intend the same content."]}],"m1-lecture07-p013":[{"text":"**Picture-book elicitation controls what speakers see, but it still cannot fully control the message they intend.** An **elicitation paradigm** (a task built to draw out speech) such as the picture book \"Frog, where are you?\" gives everyone the same scene.","children":["The scene contains a boy, a dog and frogs.","Descriptions can be compared because the visible material is held constant."]},{"text":"The intended message is still hard to define.","children":["Speakers can focus on different characters or actions.","Speakers can assume different knowledge in the audience.","Age, culture and audience may change how the message is built."]}],"m1-lecture07-p014":[{"text":"**The functional level selects lemmas rather than finished words, because a moved word can still change its grammatical form afterward.** A **lemma** is a word's identity before its final form is set.","children":["The term is borrowed from mathematics, where it means a proposition in an argument."]},{"text":"One error explains why.","children":["Intended was \"Where'd I put it when I brought it in.\"","Produced was \"Where'd I bring it when I put it in.\""]},{"text":"The verbs switched places, but the tense was applied later.","children":["\"Where'd\" already contains the past-tense helper did, so the next verb takes the base form.","So BRING came out as \"bring,\" not \"brought.\"","If the word \"brought\" had been selected, it would have moved unchanged.","PUT looks the same in both forms, so BRING gives the visible evidence."]}],"m1-lecture07-p015":[{"text":"**People often say the opposite of what they mean because opposites are closely related in the lexical system.** This is an **error in lemma selection**.","children":["A speaker said \"I really like to\" and corrected it to hate.","A speaker said bottom and meant the top of the stack of books.","A speaker said \"This room is too damn hot\" and corrected it to cold.","A speaker said oral and meant the written part of the exam."]},{"text":"Each pair shares a domain.","children":["Hot and cold share temperature.","Top and bottom share position.","Oral and written share exam format."]},{"text":"The wrong word fits the sentence.","children":["So the sentence stays well formed while its meaning is reversed."]}],"m1-lecture07-p016":[{"text":"**Antonyms are quite similar at several levels, so activation spreads easily from a lemma to its antonym.** **Antonyms** (opposite words) overlap in four ways.","children":["They are **semantically related**.","They are **strongly associated**.","They can take on the **same semantic roles**.","They belong to the **same word class**, so they can take on the same syntactic roles."]},{"text":"The first two give the antonym strong activation.","children":["In the spreading activation framework, this is a perfect storm."]},{"text":"The last two let the antonym pass through.","children":["The rest of the speech production system can handle it.","So \"hot\" for \"cold\" still makes a normal sentence."]}],"m1-lecture07-p017":[{"text":"**A blend combines parts of two competing words into one produced form.** **Lewis Carroll** made such blends on purpose and called them portmanteau words.","children":["In The Hunting of the Snark, he explains \"frumious\" from \"fuming\" and \"furious.\"","A speaker leaning toward fuming says \"fuming-furious.\"","A speaker leaning toward furious says \"furious-fuming.\"","A perfectly balanced mind says \"frumious.\""]},{"text":"**Fromkin** quoted Carroll because he said blends occur in natural speech.","children":["Carroll also proposed a hypothesis about how they occur."]},{"text":"Fromkin found that complete blends like this are seldom found in normal speech.","children":["A blend of [fjurijəs] and [fjumiŋ] is more apt to come out as [fjumijəs] or [fjuriŋ].","This is because the first syllables are identical."]}],"m1-lecture07-p018":[{"text":"**Spontaneous blends show that two alternative words can both stay active until they mix in the output.**","children":["\"I swindged\" blends **switch** and **changed**.","Other blends mix **mainly** and **mostly**, **bother** and **trouble**, and **swinging** and **hip**.","Others mix **spirant** and **fricative**, and **player** and **athlete**."]},{"text":"**Fromkin** proposed how this happens.","children":["The speaker selects words by matching **semantic features** to the meaning.","With several alternatives, both go into a **buffer** (a temporary storage compartment) with their sound specifications.","Then one is selected, or the two are blended.","These errors seem to support Carroll's assumptions."]},{"text":"Speech production nearly always has a number of alternative possibilities.","children":["Choosing among them is an ordinary part of speaking."]}],"m1-lecture07-p019":[{"text":"**Semantic roles stay the same when the sentence form changes, so lemmas can be slotted into roles before the word order is set.**","children":["In \"The plums in the icebox were eaten by me,\" the order is Undergoer, Location, Verb, Agent.","In \"I have eaten the plums in the icebox,\" the order is Agent, Verb, Undergoer, Location."]},{"text":"At the functional level, the lemmas fill **semantic roles** (also called functional roles).","children":["The verb is TO EAT.","The **agent** (the one acting) is ME.","The **undergoer** (the thing acted on) is PLUM.","The **location** is the icebox."]},{"text":"The order of roles has syntactic consequences.","children":["ME becomes \"I\" in the active sentence and \"me\" after \"by\" in the passive.","That choice happens later, at the **positional level**."]}],"m1-lecture07-p020":[{"text":"**The functional level does a lot of work, yet the speaker is still nowhere near putting a clause together, much less saying it.** By this point, three things are done.","children":["A **message** has been decided.","Appropriate **lemmas** have been selected.","The lemmas have been linked with **semantic roles**."]},{"text":"Much is still undecided.","children":["Whether the sentence is active or passive is not set.","How the pronouns will be expressed is not set.","The full sound sequence is not set."]}],"m1-lecture07-p021":[],"m1-lecture07-p022":[],"m1-lecture07-p023":[{"text":"**Many speech errors go undetected because top-down effects in perception fill in the expected message.**","children":["Listeners use context and expectations to recover what was meant.","So a real error can pass without notice.","A similar process is assumed for many malapropisms and mondegreens."]},{"text":"Some \"errors\" are made on purpose for **stylistic** reasons.","children":["**Paul Krugman** wrote \"the upper hand is on the other foot,\" quoting Hot Shots Part Deux.","It mixes \"the upper hand\" with the shoe being on the other foot."]},{"text":"A deliberate error can be **more memorable** than the correct version.","children":["The odd mismatch draws attention to the wording itself.","Recognizing both familiar phrases makes the mix funny.","So whether an error was intentional matters when it is used as evidence."]}],"m1-lecture08-p001":[],"m1-lecture08-p002":[{"text":"**Below the functional level, three levels turn chosen words into spoken movements.**","children":["The **positional level** places the chosen words into a grammatical structure.","The **phonological encoding level** turns them into a string of **phonemes** (speech sounds that tell words apart).","The **speech gestural level** turns that string into movements made by the motor system."]},{"text":"Speech errors support **abstract units**.","children":["**Phonemes**, **lemmas** and **noun phrases** cannot be heard directly.","Errors move them or leave them in place in regular ways, so the units earn a place in the theory."]},{"text":"Collected errors can be sorted by level.","children":["Purely **observational research** has weaknesses.","So **experimental methods** are needed too."]}],"m1-lecture08-p003":[{"text":"**The functional level knows the verbs and their roles, and the positional level must express them in syntax.** The sentence is the error \"Where'd I bring it when I put it in?\" It was earlier evidence that **lemmas** (words chosen for meaning and grammar, before their sounds) are useful.","children":["The functional level knows there are two verbs, PUT and BRING.","It knows I/ME is the **Agent** (the doer) of both verbs.","It knows IT is the **Undergoer** (the thing acted on) of both verbs."]},{"text":"Other facts are still open.","children":["The **temporal relationship** between the verbs is not yet set. The word when expresses it.","The question form and the verb tenses are not yet set.","The **positional level** expresses all of these in syntax."]}],"m1-lecture08-p004":[{"text":"**The positional level builds a frame with slots, and each slot sets the form of the verb that fills it.** Without the question steps, the frame is roughly \"Where'd I [verb, root form] it when I [verb, past] it in?\"","children":["The first clause needs the **base form**. It took real syntax, with **Wh- movement** and an **auxiliary verb**.","The when clause needs the **past form**.","The frame also holds the other **particles** the grammar requires."]},{"text":"The error arises while lemmas from the functional level fill the slots.","children":["BRING went into the root form slot, so it came out as bring.","PUT went into the past slot, so it came out as put.","Each verb took the form of the slot it landed in."]}],"m1-lecture08-p005":[{"text":"**In \"a language needer learns,\" two lemmas switched like bring and put, but the noun shows something new.** The intended phrase is \"a language learner needs.\"","children":["Similar, because LEARN and NEED got **switched** while being fed into the positional level.","Similar, because the frame stayed mostly the same, a noun phrase then a verb."]},{"text":"Different, because the noun is a **verb plus an affix**.","children":["Learner is learn plus -er.","Needer is need plus -er.","Adding -er to a verb is **derivational morphology** (making a new word from an existing one)."]}],"m1-lecture08-p006":[{"text":"**The roots NEED and LEARN swapped slots, but the endings stayed in their slots.** The positional level itself stayed mostly the same.","children":["The noun slot is a **verb plus -er**. This **derivational affix** (an ending that makes a new word) turns a verb into a doer noun.","The verb slot asks for the **third person singular** form with -s. This is **inflectional morphology** (an ending set by grammar)."]},{"text":"The tree shows where each ending lives.","children":["The noun phrase (NP) holds the noun, built as a verb plus -er.","The verb phrase (VP) holds a third person singular verb."]},{"text":"The result follows from the slots.","children":["Need landed in the noun slot, so it became needer.","Learn landed in the verb slot, so it became learns.","If whole words had swapped, the endings would have moved too, so the affixes are **stranded**."]}],"m1-lecture08-p007":[{"text":"**\"He did that easy enoughly\" puts the ending -ly on the wrong word.** The intended phrase is \"He did that easily enough.\"","children":["In easily, -ly attaches to easy.","In the error, -ly attaches to enough.","Easy is left bare, and the odd form enoughly appears."]},{"text":"Two questions follow.","children":["How is this error similar to \"a language needer learns\"?","How is it different?"]},{"text":"Answering both means comparing the intended and produced forms word by word.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture08-p008":[{"text":"**\"Easy enoughly\" shares a stranded affix with \"needer,\" but it also changed how the positional frame was set up.**","children":["Similar, because both have a **stranded affix** (an ending that stays in its slot while the root changes).","Here the affix is **-ly**, which makes an **adjective** into an **adverb**."]},{"text":"Different, because the frame itself changed.","children":["In \"needer,\" the positional frame stayed the same.","In \"enoughly,\" something went wrong **while setting up the positional level**."]},{"text":"Two adjective phrase (AP) trees compare the frames.","children":["The intended frame has the adverb slot first, built as adjective plus -ly, then the adjective slot.","The starred error frame has the adjective slot first, then the adverb slot.","Easy filled the first slot and enough filled the second, so enough got the -ly."]}],"m1-lecture08-p009":[{"text":"**Errors like needer and enoughly show that affixes float around at the positional level, so derivational morphology is real.**","children":["Another view says derived words are just separate stored words linked to their roots by rule-like relations.","The lexicon would hold sets like needy, need and needless, or unlearn, learn and learner.","Then affixes would need no separate representation, and no quirky word-building rules would be needed."]},{"text":"But errors make **non-words** like \"needer\" and \"enoughly\" on the fly. They suggest three things.","children":["**Lemmas and affixes** are represented **separately**.","**Affixes have a slot** in the positional level.","**Derived words** can be built by a **grammatical process**."]}],"m1-lecture08-p010":[{"text":"**An affix added at the positional level still leaves phonological work, because the sounds of the new word must be worked out.**","children":["Easy /’i.zi/ becomes easily /’i.zIl.i/.","Enough /i.’nəf/ becomes enoughly /i.’nəf.li/."]},{"text":"Some derivations change the base too.","children":["The word certificate is /sər.ˈtI.fɨ.kɨt/.","The word certification is /ˌsər.tɨ.fɨ.ˈkeI.ʃən/.","The word recertification is /ˈri.sər.tɨ.fɨ.ˌkeI.ʃən/.","**Vowels change** and **stress moves** across these forms."]},{"text":"The positional level only names a process like adding -er or making an adjective into an adverb.","children":["A later level still turns that plan into sounds."]}],"m1-lecture08-p011":[{"text":"**Phonological encoding turns lemmas, affixes and particles into a string of phonemes, and its errors reorder phonemes neatly.**","children":["For \"easy enoughly,\" this level worked fine.","The speaker did not say \"easy /inəvɪli/\" or garble the phoneme sequence."]},{"text":"Errors at this level reorder phonemes.","children":["Fish grotto came out as gish frotto.","Chomsky and Halle came out as Chomsky and Challe.","With this ring I do wed came out as with this wing I do red."]},{"text":"Menn calls these errors **\"neat.\"**","children":["They follow English **phonotactic patterns** (rules for allowed sound sequences).","As far as anyone knows, they are **clearly produced**."]}],"m1-lecture08-p012":[{"text":"**The speech gestural level carries out the planned phonemes as movements, and its errors make speech unclear.**","children":["The **motor system** carries out a series of **gestures**.","Unlike phonological encoding errors, these errors are **not necessarily \"neat.\"**","They lead to **unclear or imprecise speech**."]},{"text":"**Many errors are ambiguous.**","children":["Ad hoc came out as odd hack, with /æd hɑk/ becoming /ɑd hæk/.","/æ/ is a low front vowel, as in bat, and /ɑ/ is a low back vowel, as in hot."]},{"text":"Two levels could explain it.","children":["It could be **metathesis** (two sounds switching places) at the phonological encoding level.","It could be a **miscuing of tongue position** at the gestural level."]}],"m1-lecture08-p013":[],"m1-lecture08-p014":[{"text":"**Some collected errors change word forms, and others change sounds.**","children":["Buyed in, for bought in, puts the regular past ending on an irregular verb. This is **morphological regularization**.","Arising came out as \"arriving,\" with one consonant changed in a similar word."]},{"text":"Two errors change sounds.","children":["In \"It's pretty close to here,\" close was said [kloʊz]. The final consonant became voiced.","Thoroughly came out as \"throfly,\" with several sounds changed."]},{"text":"Each error is placed by comparing intended and produced sounds.","children":["The unit that changed points to the level."]}],"m1-lecture08-p015":[{"text":"**Some collected errors move whole words, and others move single sounds.**","children":["In \"Wash my teeth and brush my face,\" the **verbs** switched and the objects stayed."]},{"text":"Some errors move sounds.","children":["Following vowel came out as \"vollowing fowel,\" with the first consonants **switched**.","Seat heaters came out as \"heat seaters,\" with the plural ending staying on seaters.","Speech-language pathology came out as \"speech plath... speech plang,\" with attempts and repairs."]},{"text":"Some bring in a familiar sequence.","children":["Lana del Rey came out as \"Marina del Rey.\"","\"For all intensive purposes\" puts different words into a familiar phrase.","\"It's in the stimulus\" is another collected error."]}],"m1-lecture08-p016":[],"m1-lecture08-p017":[{"text":"**In a talk about hot yoga, \"a hundred-dollar room\" is an error in choosing a word for the meaning.** The speaker said \"you enter a hundred-dollar room and you do yoga.\"","children":["In a talk about cost, the phrase would be normal.","In a talk about **hot yoga**, the hundred could describe the room's heat, not its price.","Dollar is a real word that does not fit the intended meaning."]},{"text":"The rest is fine.","children":["The clause is **grammatical**.","The words are easy to pronounce.","So the error is in **word choice**, not in sounds or movements."]}],"m1-lecture08-p018":[{"text":"**\"Law and Order SUV\" switches the last two letters of SVU into a familiar abbreviation.**","children":["**SVU** belongs to the show's title.","**SUV** is a familiar vehicle abbreviation.","The same three letters appear in a new order."]},{"text":"The produced form is familiar.","children":["So a competing familiar form may have been **selected**.","The switch should not be assumed to be a simple motor slip.","The result is still easy to say."]}],"m1-lecture08-p019":[{"text":"**\"He cooked his kitchen\" for chicken mixes sound similarity with meaning context.**","children":["**In sound**, kitchen and chicken are very similar, with first and middle consonants in opposite positions.","**In meaning**, both relate to cooking."]},{"text":"The grammar stayed intact.","children":["Both words are nouns that fit the slot after his.","So the sentence keeps its **grammatical frame**.","The analysis has to use both the word forms and the situation."]}],"m1-lecture08-p020":[{"text":"**What an observer listens for and expects can change which speech errors get heard and reported.**","children":["Observers listen for the kinds of errors they expect.","Learning about speech production changes those expectations.","So expectations can affect what is heard and what is reported."]},{"text":"Placing an error in the model needs **context**.","children":["The analyst must reconstruct the speaker's intended message.","The analyst must know the situation of the error.","A form can be fine alone but wrong for what the speaker meant."]}],"m1-lecture08-p021":[{"text":"**Speech perception biases which errors get noticed, so observers may miss whole classes of errors.** This is **theory-dependent observation**, as philosophers of science call it.","children":["Listeners are poor at hearing sounds **\"halfway\" between two phonemes**, especially **consonants**.","Listeners are **biased against** hearing **phonotactically illegal strings** (sound sequences the language does not allow).","Listeners are **biased toward** hearing ambiguous sounds **as words**, so something between \"giss\" and \"kiss\" sounds more like \"kiss.\""]},{"text":"**Experimental methods** and careful measurement help.","children":["They fix some of these problems.","They do not fix all of them."]}],"m1-lecture08-p022":[],"m1-lecture08-p023":[],"m1-lecture09-p001":[],"m1-lecture09-p002":[{"text":"**Frequent object names are produced faster, but frequent words are also shorter, so frequency is confounded with length.** **Oldfield and Wingfield (1965)** asked people to name pictures of objects.","children":["They found a **log-linear relationship** (a straight line once frequency is on a logarithmic scale) between frequency and response time.","More frequent names had shorter response times.","The example line drawings come from a later set by Snodgrass and Vanderwart (1980)."]},{"text":"Frequency is related to **word length**, even in this tiny sample.","children":["A **confound** (a second variable that changes along with the tested one) gives another explanation for the result.","Either frequency or length could cause the faster naming.","A web-based tool lets researchers check this relationship with numbers."]}],"m1-lecture09-p003":[{"text":"**A lexical database lets researchers measure the properties of candidate words instead of guessing.** The **English Lexicon Project** is one such database.","children":["It gives **lexical characteristics** (properties of words such as frequency and length).","It gives **behavioral data** from naming and lexical-decision tasks."]},{"text":"Its homepage has two kinds of links.","children":["One kind generates word and nonword lists.","The other kind gives trial-level lexical-decision and naming data.","The first serves choosing stimuli, and the second serves studying responses."]},{"text":"Measurements reveal hidden differences.","children":["A list that seems \"frequent\" may also differ in length.","The database exposes that difference before the materials are chosen."]}],"m1-lecture09-p004":[{"text":"**The query tools can either build a new word list or look up the properties of an existing one.** Both ways make stimulus properties explicit.","children":["**Generate Lists of Items with Specific Lexical Characteristics** builds a new list.","**Submit lists of Items to retrieve Lexical Characteristics** looks up an existing list."]},{"text":"Each way fits a different starting point.","children":["Generating a list helps when a study needs a certain range of frequency or length.","Submitting a list helps when the words were chosen for another reason, such as a semantic category.","Either way can reveal an unintended difference between conditions."]},{"text":"Stimulus design often moves back and forth between searching and checking.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p005":[{"text":"**A query separates the properties to report from the limits on which words enter the list.** The reported properties are **fields**, and the limits are **constraints**.","children":["Checkboxes request fields such as **length** and frequency measures.","Minimum and maximum boxes constrain the chosen frequency field."]},{"text":"Retrieving both length and frequency reveals whether they change together.","children":["The frequency constraints pick candidate words.","The returned lengths tell whether more selection is needed."]},{"text":"The researcher turns a design goal into measurable criteria.","children":["The database does not choose the comparison by itself.","The chosen fields decide which confounds can be checked."]}],"m1-lecture09-p006":[{"text":"**English word frequency drops off very steeply, and length goes up as frequency goes down.** The 200 most frequent words of an old corpus were put into ten \"bins\" of twenty words each.","children":["Mean frequency drops very dramatically from the top bin to the next.","This steep drop is **Zipf's law** (a small number of words account for a large share of usage)."]},{"text":"Length is **inversely related** to frequency (as one goes up, the other goes down).","children":["The most frequent bins have the shortest words on average.","Less frequent bins have longer words."]},{"text":"This is why frequency and length are hard to separate.","children":["Picking very frequent words also picks many short words."]}],"m1-lecture09-p007":[{"text":"**High-frequency words are much shorter than low-frequency words chosen at random.** The comparison used the **467 most frequent words (HF)** and **467 random low-frequency words (LF)**.","children":["A **boxplot** (a summary of a whole distribution) shows each group's lengths.","The **median** (the middle value) of LF is higher than that of HF."]},{"text":"**Fewer than 25%** of HF words are as long as the median LF word.","children":["The difference covers most of each sample.","It does not come from one odd word."]},{"text":"Random sampling does not fix the confound.","children":["Random LF words keep the natural link between frequency and length."]}],"m1-lecture09-p008":[{"text":"**Low-frequency words should be chosen deliberately, not at random, so that length can be matched.** HF words are generally shorter than LF words.","children":["So random LF words will always be longer."]},{"text":"The fix is to pick from where the two distributions overlap.","children":["Select long-ish HF words.","Select short-ish LF words.","The groups then **match on length** while still differing in frequency."]},{"text":"Matching has limits.","children":["It changes only the experimental sample.","It does not claim frequency and length are unrelated in the language.","The final groups must be checked on both frequency and length."]}],"m1-lecture09-p009":[{"text":"**Oldfield and Wingfield matched their words for length and still found a robust frequency effect.** They concluded that **frequency effects are not merely length effects in disguise**.","children":["If length were the whole cause, matching it would remove the naming difference.","The difference stayed.","So frequency affects naming beyond length."]},{"text":"Much psycholinguistics follows this logic.","children":["Researchers try to show an effect is not just an effect of a **confounding variable** (one that changes along with the variable of interest).","Controlling the confound turns a correlation into a real test."]}],"m1-lecture09-p010":[{"text":"**Matching on many dimensions is good design, but it can leave odd, artificial and small sets of words.** **Cutler (1981)** called this \"Making up materials is a confounded nuisance.\"","children":["**Cortese and Simpson (2000)** did a very good matching job on regular and irregular words.","They matched frequency, familiarity, imageability, length and sound and spelling measures."]},{"text":"Fine-grained matching has costs.","children":["The chosen items may be somehow \"odd.\"","Their statistics may be inappropriate.","The frequency-length confound is a fact about English and most languages.","Avoiding it creates a somewhat **artificial stimulus environment**.","Few items may be left. This study had ten words per cell, which is small for word naming."]}],"m1-lecture09-p011":[{"text":"**Most interesting psycholinguistic effects happen below conscious awareness, so the tasks can be simple.** A person naming line drawings does not usually think about word frequency or length.","children":["The effects still show up in naming times."]},{"text":"Psycholinguists need less craftiness than some researchers.","children":["Researchers studying **racial stereotyping** need craftier designs.","Researchers studying **altruism** also need craftier designs."]},{"text":"**Norming studies** (studies that collect ratings of words) just ask people to rate words on some dimension.","children":["Researchers hope for the best.","Some studies still add **fillers** (extra items mixed in with the test items)."]}],"m1-lecture09-p012":[{"text":"**Good dependent measures capture behavior that participants do not consciously control.** A **dependent variable** is the outcome a researcher measures.","children":["**Eye-tracking** records where and when people look.","**EEG** and **fMRI** measure brain activity.","**Reaction time** and **accuracy** are usually not under direct control either.","A **tachistoscope** (19th century) presented stimuli briefly."]},{"text":"Brain activity is useful mainly because participants cannot usually control it.","children":["It matters less for what it says about the brain itself.","Different brain responses to two kinds of stimuli, with no overt task, suggest the stimuli differ to the participant."]}],"m1-lecture09-p013":[],"m1-lecture09-p014":[{"text":"**Belke, Meyer and Damian (2005) found that naming is slower when the objects in a set are semantically related.** They used **cyclic semantic blocking** (naming the same sets of objects again and again).","children":["**Homogeneous** sets had related names.","**Heterogeneous** sets had unrelated names."]},{"text":"Three experiments located the cost.","children":["**Experiment 1** found blocking after the objects had been named once.","**Experiment 2** showed objects together. Blocking appeared in naming latency and gaze duration, but not significantly in the gaze-to-speech lag.","**Experiment 3** found blocking for new names from the same categories."]},{"text":"The study has nice design features.","children":["It varies presentation, adds eye tracking and adds new items."]}],"m1-lecture09-p015":[{"text":"**Roelofs' WEAVER++ model describes producing a single word, so it only roughly matches a general sentence-production model.** WEAVER++ has **conceptual**, **syntactic** and **word-form** levels.","children":["The general model has message, functional and positional levels, then phonological encoding and articulation."]},{"text":"The syntactic levels differ.","children":["WEAVER++ focuses on activation for single words.","Its syntactic information is not \"positional.\"","It holds single-word features such as number and gender."]},{"text":"The conceptual and **lemma** levels matter most for blocking.","children":["A lemma is the word candidate with its syntactic features.","Word-form encoding comes after lemma selection."]}],"m1-lecture09-p016":[{"text":"**Spreading activation can make naming slower, not just faster.** In a **spreading-activation model**, related concepts share connections.","children":["Activating **duck** also activates features such as animal, bird and swim.","Those features link to **fish**, **snake** and **mouse**.","Each concept sends activation to its lemma."]},{"text":"A naive view predicts faster naming.","children":["A block of similar pictures would make every name more active."]},{"text":"The model predicts slower naming in some cases.","children":["Naming requires choosing one candidate.","A related set makes the target more available.","It also makes the competitors more available."]}],"m1-lecture09-p017":[{"text":"**Activating related words helps them become available but slows down selecting the right lemma.** Spreading activation from **duck** to **fish** means activating one helps activate the other.","children":["This causes problems when one lemma must be selected from many active ones."]},{"text":"**WEAVER++** sets selection time by a **ratio**.","children":["The top is the activity of the most active lemma.","The bottom is the overall activation of all lemmas.","A bigger total makes the ratio smaller and selection slower."]},{"text":"Feedback from the **category level** adds **collateral activation** (extra activation for other items in the same category).","children":["This raises the total activation.","So lemma selection slows down."]}],"m1-lecture09-p018":[{"text":"**The same items were named in related and unrelated blocks across eight cycles.** In each **cycle**, all of the stimuli were presented.","children":["**Homogeneous (HOM)** blocks had related items, such as \"duck\", \"mouse\", \"fish\", \"snake\".","**Heterogeneous (HET)** blocks had unrelated items, such as \"duck\", \"saw\", \"chair\", \"bus\"."]},{"text":"The same stimulus switched blocks.","children":["\"Duck\" appeared in HOM and HET blocks in alternating cycles.","So an item is HOM or HET only because of the other items in its set."]},{"text":"The design allows two comparisons.","children":["Across cycles, response times change with repetition.","Between blocks, response times differ with semantic context."]}],"m1-lecture09-p019":[{"text":"**Two effects happen at once, a big identity priming effect and a semantic blocking effect.** They come from different comparisons.","children":["**Identity priming** (faster naming of an item named before) appears across cycles.","Both HOM and HET times drop sharply after the first cycle."]},{"text":"**Semantic blocking** (slower naming in related blocks) appears between conditions.","children":["In later cycles, HOM is slower than HET."]},{"text":"The effects do not contradict each other.","children":["A condition can get faster over time.","It can still stay slower than the other condition."]}],"m1-lecture09-p020":[{"text":"**Belke and colleagues ruled out visual similarity as the cause of blocking in two ways.** Related objects might look alike, so blocking could arise in **visual object recognition**.","children":["First, the stimuli were **normed** so that same-category objects were not more visually similar on average than different-category objects.","Second, a **control experiment** blocked visual similarity."]},{"text":"The control found a different result.","children":["Visual-similarity blocking appeared in the first block of trials.","Semantic blocking appeared only after the first cycle."]},{"text":"So the effects in Experiment 1 are not likely due to visual similarity.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p021":[{"text":"**Belke and colleagues tested whether blocking depends on seeing objects one at a time.** **Hermens and colleagues** showed several objects simultaneously and found no effect.","children":["Maybe something is odd about seeing objects in serial."]},{"text":"The new task separated seeing from speaking.","children":["Participants viewed **displays of four items simultaneously**.","They produced the names in serial.","One display held duck, snake, fish and mouse."]},{"text":"Blocking still appeared.","children":["It showed up in naming latency and gaze duration.","So the effect does not depend on serial viewing."]}],"m1-lecture09-p022":[{"text":"**Eye tracking splits the naming of each object into separate time intervals.** Belke and colleagues measured **eye movements** during naming.","children":["The participant looks at one object.","The eyes move on to the next object.","The first name is spoken after the eyes have moved."]},{"text":"The intervals have labels.","children":["**GD** is **gaze duration** (time spent looking at an object).","**Art** is the onset of articulating the name.","The **lag** is the time from the end of looking to the start of the name."]},{"text":"These intervals can be compared across HOM and HET displays.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p023":[{"text":"**Gaze duration tracks lemma selection, and lag tracks the later steps that need no visual support.** Both are **dependent variables** in the study.","children":["**Gaze duration** is the time spent looking at an object.","There is evidence that **lemma selection** is coordinated with eye movements."]},{"text":"The **lag** is the time after the eyes leave an object and before its name begins.","children":["**Phonological encoding** (building the sound form) happens then.","**Gesture planning** (planning the speech movements) happens then.","These need no visual support once the eyes have moved on."]},{"text":"The two measures split stages that overall naming time combines.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p024":[{"text":"**Eye-mouth coordination links where a speaker looks to the words the speaker says.** **Griffin, Bock and colleagues** made demonstrations of it.","children":["One example is accurately labeling a patient in \"The horse is kicking a cow.\"","The **agent** (the one acting) is the horse.","The **patient** (the one affected) is the cow."]},{"text":"The eye record is lined up with the spoken words.","children":["It reveals when each part of the event matters for building the sentence.","It extends object naming to whole sentences."]}],"m1-lecture09-p025":[{"text":"**Of the three eye-tracking measures, only gaze duration shows a blocking effect.** The three measures had different results.","children":["**Overall speech duration** decreases over the experiment but shows no blocking effect.","**Gaze duration** shows blocking, but only after the first cycle.","**Lag** shows no effect."]},{"text":"The lag result needs care.","children":["Numerically, its blocking effect seems to go in the opposite direction.","That difference is not significant."]},{"text":"Practice and semantic context are separate.","children":["A measure can shrink across cycles without differing between HOM and HET."]}],"m1-lecture09-p026":[{"text":"**The gaze-duration result places semantic blocking at lemma selection.** Each measure maps onto a production stage.","children":["**Speech duration** maps onto the **gesture level** and shows no blocking.","**Gaze duration** maps onto **lemma selection** and shows blocking after the first cycle.","**Lag** maps onto **phonological encoding** and shows no significant blocking."]},{"text":"Longer looking in HOM displays fits more competition among related lemmas.","children":["No effect on lag gives no evidence of a cost after the eyes move."]}],"m1-lecture09-p027":[{"text":"**Blocking generalizes to new items from the same category.** **Experiment 3** introduced **new items** in **Cycle 5**.","children":["The repetition priming effect went away in Cycle 5.","Overall performance slowed down dramatically.","The blocking effect was still there."]},{"text":"The new items can be compared to the items in Cycle 1.","children":["In Cycle 1, nothing had been named yet, and there was no blocking.","In Cycle 5, the new items came from categories already named, and blocking appeared."]},{"text":"So blocking comes from the semantic category, not from repeating particular names.","children":[]}],"m1-lecture09-p028":[{"text":"**Belke and colleagues isolate the blocking effect by ruling out three other sources.** Each source is a different point between seeing an object and saying its name.","children":["It is not **visual recognition**.","It is not **phonological encoding**, since the words are not similar in sound.","It is not **item-specific**, since it generalizes to new items."]},{"text":"Together these controls narrow the explanation.","children":["What remains is **lemma selection** among semantically activated competitors."]}],"m1-lecture09-p029":[{"text":"**Each rival explanation for blocking has its own test.** The tests point to lemma selection.","children":["**Visual recognition** is ruled out by normed and matched stimuli and a control experiment.","**Phonological encoding** is ruled out, with a question mark, because the words are not phonologically similar. Gaze duration is affected, but eye-speech lag is not.","**Repeating specific lists** is ruled out because the effect generalizes to new items."]},{"text":"The question mark reflects that timing measures support an interpretation.","children":["They do not directly observe a hidden stage."]}],"m1-lecture09-p030":[{"text":"**A good experiment idea starts by asking whether an effect could just be something else.** The question to ask is \"Couldn’t it just be …?\" For **stimulus manipulations**, many word features are correlated with frequency.","children":["**Length** is one.","**Age of acquisition** is another.","**Concreteness** or **imageability** is another.","**Contextual diversity** is another.","So frequency effects might be a catch-all for these features."]},{"text":"For **experimental manipulations**, even a simple blocking study involves many processes.","children":["**Object recognition** is one.","**Response selection** is another."]},{"text":"Each alternative must predict a different result.","children":["Matching, direct manipulation, new items and new measures test those predictions."]}],"m1-lecture09-p031":[],"m1-review-p001":[],"m1-review-p002":[{"text":"**The Midterm 1 exam is ten short, open-book essay questions with lots of partial credit.**","children":["About 100 to 250 words should be enough to answer each question.","Each question has three or four **gradeable items**.","Each item earns credit separately, so a partly right answer still scores.","The exam is **open book**, so names and dates do not need to be memorized."]},{"text":"Preparation means explaining concepts.","children":["Explain each idea with its example and the evidence behind it."]}],"m1-review-p003":[],"m1-review-p004":[{"text":"**The consonant chart sorts every consonant by place, manner and voicing.**","children":["**Place of articulation** (where the vocal tract closes) runs along the horizontal axis, from front to back.","**Manner of articulation** (the degree of closure) runs along the vertical axis, from closed to open.","**Voicing** (when the vocal folds start vibrating) separates the two sounds in each pair."]},{"text":"Voicing pairs differ in timing.","children":["In the voiced member, voicing starts before or during the closure.","In the voiceless member, voicing starts after the closure."]},{"text":"The chart reads from front to back.","children":["Bilabial sounds like p and b sit at the left.","Velar and other back sounds sit farther right."]}],"m1-review-p005":[{"text":"**/p/ and /b/ share place and manner, and differ only in voicing.** Features describe sounds in a precise and well-defined way.","children":["/p/ is plus **plosive**, plus **bilabial**, minus voiced.","/b/ is plus plosive, plus bilabial, plus voiced."]},{"text":"The two sounds share two features.","children":["They have the same **manner** (both are plosives, with a full closure that is released).","They have the same **place** (both are bilabial, made at the lips)."]},{"text":"They differ in one feature.","children":["That feature is **voicing** (the timing of phonation relative to the release of the closure).","**Phonation** is vibration of the vocal folds."]},{"text":"The same contrast repeats elsewhere.","children":["/t/ and /d/ differ in the same way.","So features allow a general class, **the voiced stops** /b/, /d/ and /g/.","This general class is helpful for phonological descriptions."]}],"m1-review-p006":[{"text":"**Minimal pairs prove that two sounds belong to different phonemes.**","children":["A **minimal pair** (two words that differ in one sound and in meaning) shows **contrast**.","Two phones are **contrastive** when swapping them changes meaning.","Contrastive phones are examples of different **phonemes**."]},{"text":"Testing for two low back vowels follows the same steps.","children":["Find two words that differ only in those two vowels.","**Caught** and **cot** work for a speaker who keeps the vowels apart.","The consonants stay the same, so the vowel carries the meaning difference.","For a speaker who merges the vowels, caught and cot sound alike and prove nothing."]},{"text":"The evidence is meaning.","children":["A change in meaning counts, not just a change in sound."]}],"m1-review-p007":[{"text":"**Minimal pairs also reveal that many English contrasts depend on the single feature of voicing.**","children":["/b/ and /p/ differ in **voicing**.","/t/ and /d/ differ in voicing.","/k/ and /g/ differ in voicing."]},{"text":"Each pair holds place and manner constant.","children":["Inside a pair, only voicing changes.","Across the pairs, the place changes from bilabial to alveolar to velar."]},{"text":"The evidence is language specific.","children":["It comes from comparing minimal pairs in each language.","A contrast that holds in English may not hold in another language."]}],"m1-review-p008":[{"text":"**Speech is a continuous signal, so phonemes are not letters lined up in the sound.** Literate people easily imagine speech as an orderly stream of letter-like units. It is nothing like that.","children":["A **spectrogram** (a display of sound energy over time) makes this clear.","Time runs left to right.","Frequency rises from bottom to top.","Darker regions mean stronger energy."]},{"text":"The sentence \"A sore back is a real pain\" has no clean breaks.","children":["Neighboring sounds blend through transitions.","One phoneme has no single fixed shape across contexts."]},{"text":"Transcription and sound differ.","children":["A transcription is a row of separate symbols.","The signal itself is continuous and overlapping."]}],"m1-review-p009":[{"text":"**Hockett (1955) compared speech to raw Easter eggs smashed in a wringer, with the hearer as an inspector.**","children":["Raw eggs of various sizes and colors ride a moving belt.","The belt carries them through a **wringer**, which smashes them and rubs them together."]},{"text":"Each part maps onto speech.","children":["Eggs before the wringer are the impulses from the **phoneme source**.","The mess after the wringer is the output of the **speech transmitter**.","The **inspector** is the **hearer**."]},{"text":"The inspector's job is only to identify.","children":["He judges from broken and unbroken yolks, spread-out whites and bits of shell.","He does not rebuild the eggs, which would be physically impossible.","So a listener **identifies** phonemes from a smeared signal without separating the pieces."]}],"m1-review-p010":[],"m1-review-p011":[{"text":"**Derivational morphology builds new words out of existing parts called morphemes.**","children":["**Morphemes** are the parts (units) that words are built from."]},{"text":"The word morphology is built this way.","children":["**morph-** is from Greek for \"form\".","**-ology** is from Greek for something like \"the study of\".","Together they mean \"the study of the forms of words\"."]},{"text":"The same process gives other words.","children":["It gives **morpheme**.","It gives **phoneme**, with **phone** as a Greek morpheme probably meaning \"speech\"."]},{"text":"The pattern can misfire.","children":["Applying it to units in text leads to **graphology**, an awkward result."]}],"m1-review-p012":[{"text":"**Derivational affixes are productive, but they apply unevenly from word to word.** Affixes differ in how widely and how regularly they apply.","children":["Stem plus **-hood** means a state of being linked to the stem noun."]},{"text":"Some -hood words fit the pattern.","children":["**Brotherhood** fits.","**Sisterhood** fits.","**Knighthood** fits oddly. Brothers share a brotherhood, but each knight has his own knighthood."]},{"text":"Some -hood words break the pattern.","children":["**Neighborhood** is a fine word, but -hood does a different job entirely.","**Friendhood** is not the usual form. Friendship is used instead, for no apparent reason.","**Enemyhood** is only a proposed form. English uses formal words like \"enmity\" or \"antagonism\" for that idea."]},{"text":"Some examples come from Liberman and Prince.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p013":[{"text":"**Zero derivation changes a word's category with no affix, yet the meaning changes in complicated ways.** **Zero derivation** (a category change with no added affix) still affects meaning.","children":["Dish, spoon, fork and table are strong associates of the noun **knife**.","As verbs or in fixed phrases, their meanings are not similar."]},{"text":"Each use has its own meaning.","children":["\"She can dish it out but she can't take it\" uses dish as a verb with a learned meaning, not a plain reference to a dish.","\"He was born with a silver spoon and a forked tongue in his mouth\" uses fixed figurative phrases.","\"They decided to table the issue after a day of inconclusive negotiations\" uses table as a verb about an issue."]},{"text":"The meanings must be learned one by one.","children":["Figurative phrases like silver spoon are studied in **Cognitive Linguistics**."]}],"m1-review-p014":[{"text":"**Adding a derivational affix often changes how the rest of the word is pronounced.** It changes the spelling too.","children":["certificate /sər.ˈtI.fɨ.kɨt/ has main stress on the second syllable.","certification /ˌsər.tɨ.fɨ.ˈkeI.ʃən/ has main stress on the fourth syllable.","recertification /ˈri.sər.tɨ.fɨ.ˌkeI.ʃən/ has main stress on the first syllable."]},{"text":"These changes affect spelling.","children":["They are one reason for **\"The Chaos\"** of English spelling.","Spelling closer to the spoken form would not necessarily read better."]},{"text":"Noun and verb pairs often differ mainly in **stress** (which syllable is most prominent).","children":["**record** changes stress between its noun and verb.","**invite** does too, as in \"He did not receive an invite\" and \"We did not invite him\".","**entrance** does too, but its noun and verb are not actually related."]}],"m1-review-p015":[{"text":"**Inflectional morphology adds grammatical information in a more predictable way than derivation.**","children":["**Inflection** (a change for number, agreement or tense) keeps the same word."]},{"text":"Inflection comes in three kinds.","children":["**Plurals** turn knife into knives, one fork into two forks, and one mouse into a lot of mice.","The **third person singular present** turns knife into knifes, I walk into she walks, and you run into he runs.","The **past** turns knife into knifed, I walk into I walked, and she sings into she sang."]},{"text":"Menn makes a simple point.","children":["He says it would be silly to have separate dictionary entries for singular and plural nouns."]},{"text":"The sound side is less regular.","children":["Forms like mice and sang show **irregularity**.","This irregularity matters for **language learning models**.","It also matters for kinds of **developmental language impairment**."]}],"m1-review-p016":[{"text":"**Syntax can require an inflected form, but no syntactic process forces a derived one.**","children":["In \"two [noun]+pl\", whatever noun fills the slot must be pluralized somehow.","A regular noun gives two forks.","An irregular noun gives two knives."]},{"text":"Derivation has no such syntactic process.","children":["This holds even for very productive derivational forms.","The closest case is the formula \"Once you [verb] it, you can un-[verb] it\".","That formula is not exactly syntax."]},{"text":"This difference has a use.","children":["It is important for models of **speech production**."]}],"m1-review-p017":[],"m1-review-p018":[{"text":"**A simple lexical network treats each word as a node that the matching word can activate.**","children":["A **node** (a lexical representation in the network) responds to one specific word."]},{"text":"The example network is very small.","children":["It has three nodes, **lion**, **tiger** and **stripes**.","The input is the printed word **LION**."]},{"text":"Two levels are involved.","children":["The printed word gives perceptual evidence.","The node stands for the word that could be recognized."]}],"m1-review-p019":[{"text":"**Activation can be partial, so a node can be weakly active before a word is recognized.**","children":["**LION** contrasts poorly with a **noise mask** (visual noise that hides the word).","The input is weak evidence.","The lion node gets only **weak activation**."]},{"text":"The node ends up partly active.","children":["It is somewhat active but not yet identified.","Its activation can keep building as evidence comes in."]}],"m1-review-p020":[{"text":"**When the word is clear enough, its node becomes active enough to count as identified.**","children":["The **signal-to-noise ratio** (SNR, the word's strength compared with the background noise) becomes very favorable.","The lion node becomes strongly active.","The network has \"identified\" lion.","Tiger and stripes stay as unidentified alternatives."]},{"text":"This models the **perceptual identification task**.","children":["Participants push a button as soon as they can identify the item.","The measure is time to identification."]}],"m1-review-p021":[{"text":"**Experience may speed up frequent words by tuning how their activation rises over time.**","children":["**Activation dynamics** (how activation rises and falls over time) are assumed in most models of this kind.","A **frequency effect** (frequent words being identified faster) can come from this tuning."]},{"text":"Each encounter with a word might change one of three things.","children":["It might raise the word's **baseline** activation.","It might strengthen its **incoming connections**, so it activates more quickly.","It might change a **threshold** parameter."]},{"text":"This is a very simple form of **learning**.","children":["Different computational models simulate frequency effects in different ways.","One is **Rumelhart and McClelland's (1986) Interactive Activation model**."]}],"m1-review-p022":[{"text":"**Repetition priming happens because activation from a recent word has not fully faded.**","children":["**Repetition priming** (faster identification of a word seen very shortly before) follows from activation dynamics."]},{"text":"The process runs in steps.","children":["Activation does not rise and fall instantly.","It falls more slowly than it rises.","The word appears again while activation is still high.","Its baseline is already higher than usual.","It reaches identification faster."]}],"m1-review-p023":[{"text":"**Two effects need links between words, so the simple network must be extended.**","children":["**Neighborhood effects** depend on how similar a word is to other words.","**Associative, semantic and phonological priming** depend on whether a similar or related word was just seen."]},{"text":"The simple network cannot explain them.","children":["Its nodes are separate.","Each node changes only its own activation.","Connections between nodes are needed for one word to affect another."]}],"m1-review-p024":[{"text":"**Spreading activation lets an active word partly activate the words it is associated with.**","children":["Association strength comes from word association **norms**.","**Connection strength** (or distance) between nodes is set by association."]},{"text":"The spread follows the connections.","children":["Activating lion sends some activation along a weighted connection to tiger.","A stronger connection passes more activation."]},{"text":"The example network links related concepts.","children":["Vehicle links to car and bus.","Fire engine links to vehicles and to fire-related concepts.","One active concept partly activates several related ones.","This partial activation is the basis of **semantic priming**."]}],"m1-review-p025":[{"text":"**Balota and Lorch found that priming through a go-between word appeared in pronunciation but not in lexical decision.**","children":["They asked whether activation spreads beyond directly associated concepts.","Prime **lion** and target **stripes** connect only through the **mediating concept** **tiger**.","Conditions were directly related, indirect through tiger, neutral and unrelated."]},{"text":"The first task was **lexical decision** (judging whether a string is a word).","children":["Direct pairs like lion and tiger sped responses.","Lion before stripes did not beat neutral or unrelated primes."]},{"text":"The second task was **pronunciation** (reading the target aloud).","children":["Direct pairs sped responses.","Lion before stripes also sped responses."]},{"text":"They drew two conclusions.","children":["Activation spreads beyond directly related concepts in semantic memory.","Features of the lexical decision task **masked** the indirect effect."]}],"m1-review-p026":[{"text":"**Activation flowing through the network lets lion prime stripes even though they are not directly linked.**","children":["Lion connects to tiger.","Tiger connects to stripes.","Lion and stripes have no direct association."]},{"text":"Activation follows a route.","children":["Activating lion activates tiger.","Tiger passes some activation on to stripes.","Stripes becomes easier to process."]},{"text":"This indirect priming through a **mediating** node is evidence for spreading activation.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p027":[{"text":"**The indirect priming result is typical psycholinguistics, with careful design, small effects and a counterintuitive finding.**","children":["**Stimulus onset asynchrony** (SOA, the time from prime onset to target onset) was 250 ms or 500 ms.","The measure was **pronunciation latency**."]},{"text":"Mean latencies across both SOAs differ by condition.","children":["Related primes gave 549 ms.","Primes linked through tiger gave 558 ms.","Neutral primes gave 577 ms.","Unrelated primes gave 575 ms."]},{"text":"The effect is small but steady.","children":["The indirect advantage is only 17 to 19 ms.","It appears at both SOAs."]},{"text":"The result is counterintuitive.","children":["Stripes and lion share no direct link.","The only route between them runs through tiger."]}],"m1-review-p028":[{"text":"**In WEAVER++, spreading activation among similar items can make naming slower, not faster.**","children":["**Belke and colleagues (2005)** used the **WEAVER++** model.","The **upper network** links duck, fish, snake and mouse through shared concepts like animal, bird, swim and scales.","The **lower lemma nodes** are the word candidates (**lemmas**) for duck, fish, snake and mouse."]},{"text":"A naive view makes one prediction.","children":["A block of similar pictures should prime each other.","Naming should get faster."]},{"text":"The model makes the opposite prediction.","children":["Naming requires **selecting** one lemma.","Similar pictures activate many lemmas at once.","The lemmas compete, so naming can get **slower**."]}],"m1-review-p029":[],"m1-review-p030":[{"text":"**Broca's patient Tan could not speak but seemed to understand what was said.**","children":["In **1861**, neurologist **Pierre Paul Broca** presented the case.","He presented it to the newly established **Society of Anthropology**.","He was also its founding president."]},{"text":"Tan lost speech but kept other abilities.","children":["Tan had lost speech after a brain injury decades earlier.","He could only produce one jargon syllable.","He seemed otherwise \"in possession of his senses\".","He seemed to understand what was being said."]},{"text":"Tan was not the first observed case of a specific loss like this.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p031":[{"text":"**Tan's large frontal lesion gave Broca's area its name, but locating damage is not locating speech.**","children":["Tan's brain is on display at the **Musee Dupuytren**.","It has a **large lesion**.","The lesion definitely includes the **inferior frontal gyrus** (the third convolution of the frontal lobe)."]},{"text":"This case gave rise to a claim.","children":["**Broca's area** is a portion of the left frontal lobe important for speech."]},{"text":"The claim drew criticism.","children":["**Hughlings Jackson (1874)** said \"to locate the damage which destroys speech and to locate speech are two different things\".","Damage in one place can stop speech without that place holding speech."]}],"m1-review-p032":[{"text":"**Lichtheim and Wernicke explained aphasias as disconnections between language centers.**","children":["In the **1870s**, these German neurologists observed **aphasia** (language loss after brain damage) more systematically.","They ascribed specific losses to **disconnections** between **centers**."]},{"text":"Lichtheim's house has three centers.","children":["A is sound images.","B is concepts.","M is movement images."]},{"text":"Each disconnection causes a different loss.","children":["A cut between A and B could cause loss of understanding spoken words.","A cut between A and M could cause loss of verbatim repetition."]},{"text":"This centers approach is an ancestor of **modularity**.","children":["Eling and Whitaker (2009) review this history."]}],"m1-review-p033":[{"text":"**Freud argued that the brain may not be organized by our psychological categories.**","children":["**Sigmund Freud** criticized the **\"diagram-drawing\" school** of aphasia research.","He thought it naive to expect brain organization to match categories like \"word forms\" or \"concepts\".","He proposed that language emerged from **fields** linking perceptual and motor abilities to **drives**."]},{"text":"This is a **holist critique**.","children":[]},{"text":"It names a durable tension in **cognitive neuroscience**.","children":["Can brain area functions be directly related to cognitive functions?","Is the brain really organized by our descriptions of cognitive phenomena?","A useful psychological category has to be tied to the brain by evidence."]}],"m1-review-p034":[{"text":"**MRI showed that Broca's famous cases had deep damage that reached far beyond Broca's area.**","children":["**Dronkers and colleagues** used **MRI** to assess Broca's landmark cases very precisely.","One of these brains is **Leborgne**'s."]},{"text":"The MRI found extensive damage.","children":["The lesion is very **deep** and includes the **insula**.","There is heavy **white matter damage** to the **superior longitudinal fasciculus** (a pathway connecting the front and back of the cerebral cortex)."]},{"text":"Other work by this group points to the insula.","children":["The insula, not Broca's area, is strongly associated with **speech apraxia**.","Speech apraxia is the condition Broca described in his patients."]}],"m1-review-p035":[],"m1-review-p036":[{"text":"**Speakers select lemmas at the functional level, and the tense of each verb is added later.**","children":["A **lemma** (a word's identity before its grammatical form is set) is what gets selected.","The term is borrowed from mathematics, where it is a proposition in an argument."]},{"text":"A speech error reveals this.","children":["The intended sentence was \"Where'd I put it when I brought it in\".","The produced sentence was \"Where'd I bring it when I put it in\"."]},{"text":"The error has a clear pattern.","children":["The verbs switched places.","The moved verb came out as \"bring\", not \"brought\".","So the tense was applied after the switch.","If \"brought\" had been selected, it would have moved unchanged.","So the lemma BRING was selected and later expressed in its present form."]}],"m1-review-p037":[{"text":"**Antonyms slip into speech easily because they overlap with the intended word at many levels.**","children":["People often say the opposite of what they mean."]},{"text":"Antonyms overlap in meaning.","children":["Antonyms are **semantically related**.","They are **strongly associated**.","In **spreading activation** terms, this is a perfect storm."]},{"text":"Antonyms also overlap in grammar.","children":["They can take on the same **semantic roles**.","They share a **word class**, so they take the same syntactic roles.","The rest of the speech production system can handle either one."]},{"text":"So activation spreads easily from a **lemma** to its antonym.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p038":[{"text":"**Semantic roles stay the same even when the sentence order changes.**","children":["**Semantic roles** (how each participant relates to the event) are also called functional roles."]},{"text":"The passive sentence has one order.","children":["\"The plums in the icebox were eaten by me\" is **Undergoer**, **Location**, Verb, **Agent**."]},{"text":"The active sentence has another order.","children":["\"I have eaten the plums in the icebox\" is Agent, Verb, Undergoer, Location."]},{"text":"Lemmas can then be slotted into roles.","children":["The verb is TO EAT.","The agent is ME.","The undergoer is PLUM."]},{"text":"Order has **syntactic consequences**.","children":["It decides whether the lemma ME becomes I or me.","That happens at the **positional level**."]}],"m1-review-p039":[{"text":"**The functional level settles the message, the lemmas and their roles, but builds no clause yet.**","children":["A **message** to convey is decided.","Appropriate **lemmas** are selected.","The lemmas are associated with **semantic roles**."]},{"text":"Much is still missing.","children":["The clause is not put together.","Word order and word forms are not set.","Nothing is said out loud."]}],"m1-review-p040":[{"text":"**The positional level turns the functional plan into syntax, including how the two events relate in time.**","children":["The example sentence is \"Where'd I bring it when I put it in?\""]},{"text":"Some facts are known after the functional level.","children":["There are two verbs, **PUT** and **BRING**.","I/ME is the **agent** of both.","IT is the **undergoer** of both."]},{"text":"Other facts are still needed.","children":["The speaker needs the **temporal relationship** between the verbs. The putting in happened in the past, so its clause needs a past form.","The speaker needs the question structure."]},{"text":"Expressing all of this syntactically is the **positional level**'s job.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p041":[{"text":"**The positional level builds a frame of slots, and each slot sets the form of the lemma that fills it.**","children":["The frame is \"Where'd I [verb, root form] it when I [verb, past] it in?\"","It also holds other particles the grammar requires."]},{"text":"The two clauses have different slots.","children":["The first clause needs **Wh- movement** and an **auxiliary verb** to form the question.","Its verb slot requires the **base form**.","The next clause's slot requires the **past form**."]},{"text":"The error happens during slot filling.","children":["\"Where'd I bring it when I put it in\" arises while slots are filled with lemmas.","BRING landed in the base slot, so it became bring.","PUT landed in the past slot, and its past form is put."]}],"m1-review-p042":[{"text":"**In \"a language needer learns,\" two roots swapped while the affixes stayed in their slots.**","children":["Two lemmas got switched when fed into the positional level.","The positional level stayed nearly the same.","This matches the bring and put error."]},{"text":"The difference is in the noun.","children":["The noun is a **verb plus an affix**, need plus -er.","Adding -er is **derivational morphology** (building a new word from parts).","The verb slot kept its **inflectional** ending -s, so learn became learns."]}],"m1-review-p043":[{"text":"**\"He did that easy enoughly\" has a stranded affix and also a reversed positional frame.**","children":["A **stranded affix** (an affix left in its slot while the root changes) appears here too.","The affix is **-ly**, which makes an adjective into an adverb."]},{"text":"The two errors share one property.","children":["Both this error and \"a language needer learns\" strand an affix."]},{"text":"This error also differs.","children":["Something also happened when setting up the **positional level**.","The intended frame is an adverb (adjective plus -ly) followed by an adjective.","The error frame is an adjective followed by an adverb (adjective plus -ly).","So easy took the plain slot and enough took the -ly."]}],"m1-review-p044":[],"m1-review-p045":[{"text":"**Oldfield and Wingfield found that objects with frequent names are named faster, but frequency is tangled with length.**","children":["**Oldfield and Wingfield (1965)** asked people to name pictures of objects.","They found a **log-linear relationship** (a straight line against the logarithm of frequency) between frequency and response time.","The line drawings come from **Snodgrass and Vanderwart (1980)**."]},{"text":"The finding has a problem.","children":["Frequency is related to **word length**, even in this tiny sample.","Frequent words tend to be shorter.","Length is a **confound** (a second variable that changes along with the studied one).","Either variable could explain naming time."]},{"text":"This is a classic cautionary tale.","children":[]}],"m1-review-p046":[{"text":"**Word frequency falls off steeply from the top words, and length rises as frequency falls.**","children":["The 200 most frequent words of an old corpus were put into ten bins of twenty."]},{"text":"Frequency changes across the bins.","children":["The first bin averages above 15,000 per million.","The second bin drops below 4,000 per million.","Later bins are much smaller, roughly 2,000 per million or less.","This steep drop is **Zipf's law** in action."]},{"text":"Length changes the other way.","children":["The first bin averages under three letters.","The last bins average about five letters.","Length is **inversely related** to frequency."]},{"text":"Real words fit this pattern.","children":["Top words like the, of, and, to and a are three letters or fewer.","Later high-frequency words like century and Washington are longer."]}],"m1-review-p047":[{"text":"**To separate frequency from length, pick the words on purpose instead of at random.**","children":["High-frequency (**HF**) words are generally shorter than low-frequency (**LF**) words.","Random LF words will always be longer."]},{"text":"The fix is deliberate selection.","children":["Choose long-ish HF words.","Choose short-ish LF words.","The groups are then **matched on length** while frequency is manipulated."]}],"m1-review-p048":[{"text":"**With length matched, the frequency effect stayed, so frequency is not just length in disguise.**","children":["**Oldfield and Wingfield** eventually matched their high and low frequency words for **length**.","They still found a **robust frequency effect**.","They concluded that frequency effects are not merely length effects in disguise."]},{"text":"This logic is general.","children":["A lot of psycholinguistics tries to show that an effect is not just a **confounding variable** at work."]}],"m1-review-p049":[],"m1-review-p050":[{"text":"**Belke, Meyer and Damian (2005) found that naming related objects over and over gets slower.**","children":["In **cyclic semantic blocking**, people repeatedly name sets of objects.","**Homogeneous sets** have semantically related names.","**Heterogeneous sets** have unrelated names.","Naming latencies are usually longer in related sets."]},{"text":"Experiment 1 found a timing pattern.","children":["The effect arose only after every object in a set had been shown and named once."]},{"text":"Experiment 2 added eye tracking.","children":["Objects of a set were shown together.","Blocking appeared in naming latencies and **gaze durations**.","The **gaze-to-speech lag** (from gaze leaving an object to saying its name) showed no blocking."]},{"text":"Experiment 3 used new items.","children":["Blocking generalized to new, previously unnamed items."]}],"m1-review-p051":[{"text":"**Activation and selection are different, so extra activation can slow down choosing a lemma.**","children":["Spreading activation from **duck** to **fish** means activating one helps activate the other.","That causes trouble when one correct **lemma** must be selected from many active ones."]},{"text":"The **WEAVER++** model has a selection rule.","children":["**Selection time** depends on a ratio.","The ratio compares the most active lemma with the total activation of all lemmas.","More active competitors make the target stand out less."]},{"text":"Category feedback creates this problem.","children":["**Feedback from the category level** adds **collateral activation** to same-category items.","This slows lemma selection."]}],"m1-review-p052":[{"text":"**Belke, Meyer and Damian (2005) found that naming related objects over and over gets slower.**","children":["In **cyclic semantic blocking**, people repeatedly name sets of objects.","**Homogeneous sets** have semantically related names.","**Heterogeneous sets** have unrelated names.","Naming latencies are usually longer in related sets."]},{"text":"Experiment 1 found a timing pattern.","children":["The effect arose only after every object in a set had been shown and named once."]},{"text":"Experiment 2 added eye tracking.","children":["Objects of a set were shown together.","Blocking appeared in naming latencies and **gaze durations**.","The **gaze-to-speech lag** (from gaze leaving an object to saying its name) showed no blocking."]},{"text":"Experiment 3 used new items.","children":["Blocking generalized to new, previously unnamed items."]}],"m1-review-p053":[{"text":"**Activation and selection are different, so extra activation can slow down choosing a lemma.**","children":["Spreading activation from **duck** to **fish** means activating one helps activate the other.","That causes trouble when one correct **lemma** must be selected from many active ones."]},{"text":"The **WEAVER++** model has a selection rule.","children":["**Selection time** depends on a ratio.","The ratio compares the most active lemma with the total activation of all lemmas.","More active competitors make the target stand out less."]},{"text":"Category feedback creates this problem.","children":["**Feedback from the category level** adds **collateral activation** to same-category items.","This slows lemma selection."]}],"m1-review-p054":[{"text":"**In Experiment 2, only gaze duration showed blocking, which supports a lemma-selection account.**","children":["**Speech duration** decreases over the experiment but shows no blocking. It reflects the **gesture level**.","**Gaze duration** shows blocking, but only after the first cycle. It reflects **lemma selection**.","**Lag** shows no effect. It reflects **phonological encoding**."]},{"text":"The lag result needs care.","children":["Numerically, the blocking effect seems to go the opposite way.","This difference is not significant."]}],"m1-review-p055":[{"text":"**Blocking generalized to new items from the same category, so it is not tied to repeating particular items.**","children":["**Experiment 3** introduced new items in **Cycle 5**."]},{"text":"Cycle 5 changed some things but not others.","children":["Naming slowed down dramatically overall.","The **repetition priming** effect went away.","The **blocking effect** stayed, with homogeneous sets slower than heterogeneous sets."]},{"text":"Cycle 5 is best compared with Cycle 1.","children":["In Cycle 1, no category had been named yet, and there was no blocking.","In Cycle 5, the items were new but their category was not, and blocking appeared."]}],"m1-review-p056":[{"text":"**Belke and colleagues ruled out three alternatives to place the blocking effect at lemma selection.**","children":["It is not **visual recognition**. The stimuli were **normed and matched**, and a **control experiment** was run.","It is probably not **phonological encoding**, which is marked with a question mark. The words were not phonologically similar. Gaze duration, not eye-speech lag, was affected.","It is not just a specific effect of **repeating lists**. Blocking generalizes to new items."]}]}};
